  ($qb$HEAIL-ISF-SJT-044$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You run a chicken shop. Your supplier's prices, the wholesale rate and what two nearby shops charge all move separately. You are deciding this week's price. How do you approach it?$qb$, $qb$Identify which of the three would actually change your price$qb$, $qb$Look at all three and take a middle position$qb$, $qb$Follow the wholesale rate, since it moves first$qb$, $qb$Match whatever the nearby shops charge$qb$, $qb$Keep your price steady and ignore the movement$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ISF-SJT-045$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You run an ice cream cart. You keep daily notes on weather, footfall, takings and flavours sold. Nothing you have written has yet changed what you do. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Decide what you would do differently, then record only that$qb$, $qb$Review the notes properly at the end of the month$qb$, $qb$Keep recording, since patterns take time to appear$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to look at the notes$qb$, $qb$Stop keeping the notes altogether$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
