  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team argues that a service must keep a seven-step approval because 'risk requires it.' No one can explain which risk each step controls. What is the best way to challenge the design?$qb$, $qb$Define the unacceptable outcomes first, identify controls actually needed for those risks, and rebuild the approval path from that requirement.$qb$, $qb$Remove the two slowest approvals and monitor whether incidents increase.$qb$, $qb$Benchmark how many approvals comparable organizations use.$qb$, $qb$Keep all seven steps until Risk formally approves a reduction.$qb$, $qb$Automate the existing approvals so the process becomes faster without redesign.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A seven-stage approval process exists because controls accumulated after unrelated incidents; nobody can explain why every stage is still needed. How should you redesign it?$qb$, $qb$Start from outcomes and risks that must be controlled, derive the minimum necessary checks, then validate the design against real failure modes.$qb$, $qb$Remove stages with the lowest rejection rates and monitor incidents.$qb$, $qb$Benchmark approval counts at comparable organizations.$qb$, $qb$Keep every stage unless the original control owner approves removal.$qb$, $qb$Automate all seven stages so the process becomes faster.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A long-standing approval exists because 'the board once asked for it,' but nobody can identify the risk it controls. What is the strongest redesign method?$qb$, $qb$Define unacceptable outcomes and obligations first, determine what control is necessary, and retain approval only if it materially addresses that need.$qb$, $qb$Introduce a sensible safeguard but leave the underlying incentives or decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Run a small corrective action focused mainly on the most visible symptom.$qb$, $qb$Ask individual managers to compensate for the problem through extra effort.$qb$, $qb$Treat the issue as unavoidable and make no material change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An inherited process has nine mandatory reviews, each justified by a different historical incident. What is the strongest redesign method?$qb$, $qb$Start with current unacceptable outcomes and obligations, derive the controls actually required, and test whether each review materially reduces relevant risk.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A control requires three senior approvals because each approver historically checks the same evidence. What is the strongest redesign?$qb$, $qb$Define the risk and necessary independent judgments, eliminate redundant checks, and assign the minimum clear authorities needed to control the outcome.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process requires a committee vote because a serious failure occurred ten years ago, but today's controls and technology are different. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Reconstruct the risk the vote was meant to control, assess current failure modes and safeguards, and retain only governance that adds material protection now.$qb$, $qb$At first, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$In this case, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team spends significant time on an old copy-paste reconciliation task. What is the best way to decide how the work should be redesigned?$qb$, $qb$Deconstructing underlying data inputs and reporting needs, eliminating redundant steps, and automating flow.$qb$, $qb$Searching for advanced spreadsheet macros to execute existing copy-paste steps more quickly.$qb$, $qb$Dividing manual copy-paste duties among multiple team members to reduce individual time burden.$qb$, $qb$Recommending that reconciliation task frequencies be reduced from weekly to monthly to save hours.$qb$, $qb$Executing the process exactly as documented to maintain historical tracking continuity.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A form asks for information that nobody uses after submission. The activity consumes recurring capacity. What is the best first question?$qb$, $qb$What purpose does this information serve?$qb$, $qb$Who added this field to the form?$qb$, $qb$How long does the field take to complete?$qb$, $qb$Can the field be made optional?$qb$, $qb$Can the form be automated?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-035$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A monthly approval report contains many sections copied from older versions, but nobody can explain why all are needed. What is the best starting point?$qb$, $qb$Define the decisions and risks the report must support, then test each section against those needs$qb$, $qb$Ask current readers which sections they actually use$qb$, $qb$Remove the sections that receive little attention$qb$, $qb$Automate the report so the extra sections cost less time$qb$, $qb$Keep the report unchanged because it supports approval$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You receive a long email thread before a meeting. What should you extract first?$qb$, $qb$The decision needed, key facts, open issues, and owners$qb$, $qb$The latest message and any attached files$qb$, $qb$A short summary of every message$qb$, $qb$Comments from the most senior people$qb$, $qb$Every detail in the order it was written$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Five people send updates on the same issue. Two contain new facts; three repeat old information. What is the best summary?$qb$, $qb$Highlight the new facts, current decision need, and any unresolved point$qb$, $qb$Summarize the two messages with new information$qb$, $qb$List all five updates in a shorter form$qb$, $qb$Give equal space to each person's update$qb$, $qb$Use only the latest message$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have data, customer comments, and a manager's opinion about the same problem. What is the best way to brief a decision?$qb$, $qb$Separate evidence from opinion, highlight the strongest signals, and state what remains uncertain$qb$, $qb$Summarize the data and comments, then note the manager's view$qb$, $qb$Give equal weight to all three sources$qb$, $qb$Lead with the manager's opinion$qb$, $qb$Present everything without prioritizing it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to summarize a problem from twenty messages, many of which repeat the same facts. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Extract the confirmed facts, key disagreement, current impact, and decision needed$qb$, $qb$Group repeated messages and summarize the newest information$qb$, $qb$Write a shorter version of all twenty messages$qb$, $qb$Quote only the most senior participants$qb$, $qb$Use the final message as the summary$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have several facts about a delay, but only two explain what decision is needed now. What should your update emphasize?$qb$, $qb$The two decision-relevant facts, current impact, and the choice required$qb$, $qb$The key facts and the immediate impact$qb$, $qb$Every fact so nobody can say information was omitted$qb$, $qb$The most recent fact only$qb$, $qb$The fact provided by the most senior person$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is the primary goal when synthesizing a dense multi-page industry report into an executive briefing?$qb$, $qb$Distilling critical strategic signals, removing operational noise, and presenting actionable insights.$qb$, $qb$Copying key executive summary sections and visual data charts directly into a condensed document.$qb$, $qb$Creating a detailed section-by-section outline covering all topics presented in the original report.$qb$, $qb$Presenting raw numerical data tables exclusively, omitting narrative context to keep briefings short.$qb$, $qb$Compiling individual chapter summaries written by department specialists into a unified document.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Finance, Sales, Operations and an external adviser provide conflicting forecasts before a capital decision. What should your executive synthesis emphasize?$qb$, $qb$Decision-relevant facts, why credible sources disagree, key assumptions and sensitivities, and uncertainty that remains material to the choice.$qb$, $qb$Average the four forecasts to create one neutral estimate.$qb$, $qb$Use the external adviser's forecast because it is independent.$qb$, $qb$Use the most conservative forecast to protect the organization.$qb$, $qb$Present all forecast details equally and let executives interpret them.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Before an acquisition vote, market research, employee interviews, customer data and a consultant report point in different directions. What should your synthesis do?$qb$, $qb$Organize evidence around critical assumptions, weight sources by relevance and reliability, expose contradictions, and show which uncertainties could change the recommendation.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate issue with a reasonable local action, but leave the broader assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather some additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask the affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or externally forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An executive briefing contains strong evidence both for and against a proposed acquisition. What should the synthesis do?$qb$, $qb$Organize evidence around the assumptions that determine value, weigh source quality, expose contradictions, and show which unresolved points could reverse the recommendation.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to advise a manager using data from several sources that partly disagree. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Reconcile material differences, distinguish strong evidence from weak signals, and state what remains uncertain$qb$, $qb$Compare the sources, explain the main differences, and identify the most reliable evidence$qb$, $qb$Average the conflicting numbers$qb$, $qb$Use the source with the most detail$qb$, $qb$Present all sources without judging their reliability$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-036$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must brief a decision-maker using operational data, customer feedback, and expert opinions that conflict on one important point. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Distinguish evidence quality, identify agreement and conflict, explain the uncertainty, and connect the strongest signals to the decision required$qb$, $qb$Summarize each source and explain where they disagree$qb$, $qb$Give equal weight to all sources$qb$, $qb$Follow the expert with the most experience$qb$, $qb$Present the information without recommending a view$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A shortcut makes your team's work faster but creates extra work for another team. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review the total impact before deciding whether to keep the shortcut$qb$, $qb$Discuss the extra work with the affected team$qb$, $qb$Keep the shortcut if your own target improves$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team to adjust their process$qb$, $qb$Ignore the extra work because it belongs to another team$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A faster intake process increases incomplete requests reaching the next team. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review the whole flow and improve intake speed without passing poor-quality work downstream.$qb$, $qb$Measure which missing details create the most rework and adjust intake.$qb$, $qb$Keep the faster intake and let the next team reject incomplete requests.$qb$, $qb$Add more staff to the next team.$qb$, $qb$Judge success only by intake speed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team reduces a checking step and finishes work faster, but customer corrections increase. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the whole flow and balance speed with the cost of corrections$qb$, $qb$Restore the check while you examine the new error pattern$qb$, $qb$Keep the faster process and handle corrections separately$qb$, $qb$Ask customer support to manage the extra corrections$qb$, $qb$Judge success only by your team's faster completion time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new shortcut saves five minutes in your task but creates ten minutes of extra checking for the next person. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Consider the total process time and change the shortcut if it makes the overall flow worse$qb$, $qb$Discuss the extra checking with the next person$qb$, $qb$Keep the shortcut because your own task is faster$qb$, $qb$Ask the next person to improve their checking method$qb$, $qb$Ignore the extra work because it happens later$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A rule prevents one type of error but creates repeated manual exceptions elsewhere. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate both the prevented errors and exception cost, then improve the rule for the whole flow$qb$, $qb$Measure the exception pattern and discuss it with affected teams$qb$, $qb$Keep the rule because it prevents errors$qb$, $qb$Add more people to handle exceptions$qb$, $qb$Remove the rule without checking the original risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new quality check reduces customer errors but creates a long queue before delivery. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Measure both error reduction and queue impact, then improve the flow without losing essential control$qb$, $qb$Find which part of the check causes the waiting and redesign it$qb$, $qb$Remove the check because the queue is visible$qb$, $qb$Keep the check unchanged because quality improved$qb$, $qb$Add more waiting time to the delivery promise$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process change makes one step faster but causes more errors later in the workflow. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Evaluating systemic trade-offs, restoring essential quality checks, and redesigning workflows to balance speed.$qb$, $qb$Adding warning documentation to deliverables advising clients to verify contents upon receipt.$qb$, $qb$Maintaining high processing speeds while establishing dedicated fast-track teams to handle downstream errors.$qb$, $qb$Reverting strictly to original slower workflows to eliminate downstream operational errors completely.$qb$, $qb$Transferring downstream error tracking metrics to support teams to handle post-delivery complaints.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A cost target causes teams to defer preventive maintenance, improving quarterly results but increasing failures elsewhere. What should executives change?$qb$, $qb$Redesign measures and accountability around lifecycle economics and cross-unit consequences so local savings cannot transfer risk.$qb$, $qb$Resolve the immediate pressure and document the remaining risk for later review.$qb$, $qb$Use a narrower solution that improves one dimension but leaves key dependencies unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Transfer the decision to another function without clarifying enterprise ownership.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current plan even when evidence shows its assumptions are weakening.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A shared technology platform reduces IT cost but forces business units into manual workarounds that are not counted centrally. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Measure total enterprise cost and service impact, surface transferred work, and adjust platform priorities and incentives based on end-to-end value.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$At first, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$In this case, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-035$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A governance system has grown through decades of risk events and is now too slow for the current business. What is the strongest redesign?$qb$, $qb$Start from today's unacceptable outcomes, obligations and decision needs, derive the minimum effective controls and authorities, and remove inherited layers that no longer add independent protection.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A change reduces your team's workload but causes more questions for customer support. A local improvement may be moving work elsewhere. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Measure the total effect and adjust the change so the overall process improves$qb$, $qb$Discuss the extra questions with customer support and identify the cause$qb$, $qb$Keep the change because your team is more efficient$qb$, $qb$Ask support to create a standard answer for the new questions$qb$, $qb$Ignore the effect because support owns customer questions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A local improvement reduces processing time but increases customer contacts and support workload. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate the total customer and operational impact, then redesign the change so the overall system improves$qb$, $qb$Measure the extra contacts and work with support to identify the cause$qb$, $qb$Keep the improvement because processing time is the team's target$qb$, $qb$Ask support to automate the extra contacts$qb$, $qb$Reverse the change immediately without measuring the benefits$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A change reduces processing cost in one team but increases error handling, customer calls, and waiting elsewhere. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate total cost, customer impact, and flow performance, then redesign the change around the whole outcome$qb$, $qb$Map the downstream effects and compare total effort before and after$qb$, $qb$Keep the change because the original team's cost fell$qb$, $qb$Ask downstream teams to absorb the extra work$qb$, $qb$Reverse the change immediately without preserving any benefit$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate says a process change caused a recent drop in quality. What should you check first?$qb$, $qb$Whether the evidence actually links the change to the quality drop$qb$, $qb$What data was used to reach the conclusion$qb$, $qb$Whether other teammates agree with the explanation$qb$, $qb$How confident the teammate sounds about the cause$qb$, $qb$Whether the explanation is easy to understand$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A survey result is based on five responses from a team of one hundred. What is the main concern?$qb$, $qb$The sample is too small to support a strong conclusion about the whole team$qb$, $qb$The five respondents may not represent the full team$qb$, $qb$The survey may contain poorly worded questions$qb$, $qb$The result may still be correct if all five agree$qb$, $qb$There is no concern if the percentage is high$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A report shows complaints fell after a change, but total customer volume also fell. What should you check?$qb$, $qb$Whether the complaint rate changed after accounting for customer volume$qb$, $qb$Compare complaints as a percentage of customer activity$qb$, $qb$Conclude the change worked because complaints are lower$qb$, $qb$Ignore customer volume because complaint count is what matters$qb$, $qb$Use whichever measure gives the better result$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A coworker says a training session improved performance because scores rose afterward. What else should you consider?$qb$, $qb$Whether other changes could explain the higher scores and whether the comparison is fair$qb$, $qb$How scores were measured before and after the training$qb$, $qb$Whether participants liked the training$qb$, $qb$Whether the trainer is experienced$qb$, $qb$Whether the improvement sounds reasonable$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a change is successful because one important customer liked it. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Look for broader evidence before concluding the change works for customers generally$qb$, $qb$Ask how other customers responded$qb$, $qb$Accept the conclusion because the customer is important$qb$, $qb$Reject the conclusion because one customer is never useful evidence$qb$, $qb$Use the customer's opinion as the main measure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A dashboard shows a large percentage increase, but the starting number was very small. What should you check?$qb$, $qb$The actual numbers as well as the percentage before judging the size of the change$qb$, $qb$Compare the starting and ending values$qb$, $qb$Focus on the percentage because it shows growth$qb$, $qb$Assume a large percentage means a large business impact$qb$, $qb$Ignore the starting value$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How does a critical thinker objectively evaluate vendor claims regarding software efficiency gains?$qb$, $qb$Scrutinizing raw performance data, assessing workflow compatibility, and requesting structured trials.$qb$, $qb$Presenting vendor claims to leadership, highlighting positive client testimonials and advertised benefits.$qb$, $qb$Requesting vendor pricing discounts before deciding whether to pilot the software tool.$qb$, $qb$Consulting other internal departments to inquire if they have utilized similar vendor tools in past projects.$qb$, $qb$Accepting vendor statistics as valid industry benchmarks and recommending software adoption.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A business case estimates major savings from consolidating vendors. The model assumes every business unit will adopt the new contract immediately. What is the strongest review?$qb$, $qb$Test adoption realism, transition costs, exceptions, switching risks and sensitivity before accepting the headline savings.$qb$, $qb$Ask the sponsor to reduce the savings estimate by a standard contingency percentage.$qb$, $qb$Compare the estimate with savings achieved in other consolidation programs.$qb$, $qb$Approve the case if the arithmetic in the model is correct.$qb$, $qb$Reject the case because organization-wide adoption is never realistic.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic review cites ten favorable market indicators but omits two credible signals that could invalidate the growth thesis. What should concern you most?$qb$, $qb$Test the thesis against the omitted signals and specify what evidence would cause the organization to change course.$qb$, $qb$Take a practical partial step and escalate the remaining uncertainty to a higher authority.$qb$, $qb$Apply the existing policy more carefully, even though it does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects the most visible short-term target.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the conflicting evidence and continue with the established position.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An investment proposal relies on three assumptions supplied by a vendor that benefits from approval. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Independently test the assumptions capable of changing the decision, examine incentives and downside cases, and separate verified evidence from interested claims.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A market-entry case uses impressive averages, but outcomes vary dramatically by customer segment. What is the strongest analytical response?$qb$, $qb$Disaggregate the economics and drivers by segment, test whether the average masks loss-making or strategically different populations, and reassess the thesis.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A proposal shows a strong average return but combines one highly profitable region with three marginal ones. What should the decision-maker do?$qb$, $qb$Disaggregate value drivers and strategic rationale by region, test dependencies and alternatives, and decide whether bundling hides materially different investments.$qb$, $qb$At first, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$In this case, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A proposal claims a new channel caused revenue growth because both began in the same quarter. What is the strongest challenge?$qb$, $qb$Test alternative explanations, timing, customer mix and counterfactual evidence before attributing the growth to the channel.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral decision that reduces visible tension while leaving the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer says a problem happens 'every time,' but you have only one recorded case. The conclusion may influence a wider decision. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check the records and gather enough evidence before accepting the claim as a pattern$qb$, $qb$Ask for examples of other times the problem occurred$qb$, $qb$Treat it as a repeated issue because the customer is confident$qb$, $qb$Assume the records are incomplete$qb$, $qb$Dismiss the claim because only one case is recorded$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team claims a new process improved quality because errors fell by half, but the number of completed cases also fell sharply. What should you examine?$qb$, $qb$Compare error rates and relevant volumes before concluding the process improved quality$qb$, $qb$Calculate errors as a proportion of completed cases$qb$, $qb$Accept the improvement because the number of errors fell$qb$, $qb$Reject the improvement because output volume fell$qb$, $qb$Use whichever measure supports the team's conclusion$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A business unit reports that a new process improved productivity, but overtime increased during the same period. What should you examine?$qb$, $qb$Whether the productivity measure still shows improvement after accounting for overtime and other resource changes$qb$, $qb$Compare output per paid hour before and after the process change$qb$, $qb$Accept the productivity gain because output increased$qb$, $qb$Reject the claim because overtime increased$qb$, $qb$Use only the reported productivity percentage$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must choose between two low-risk options today, but one useful fact will arrive tomorrow. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose a reversible step using current evidence and review it tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Identify how much the missing fact could change the decision$qb$, $qb$Delay all action until the missing fact arrives$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that worked last time$qb$, $qb$Ask someone senior to make the decision$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A low-cost decision must be made today, but your information is incomplete. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose the safest reversible option supported by current facts$qb$, $qb$Identify the biggest unknown and limit its possible impact$qb$, $qb$Wait until you have complete information$qb$, $qb$Choose what most coworkers would choose$qb$, $qb$Avoid deciding and let the situation resolve itself$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to choose a low-cost supplier for a trial, but one reference check is still pending. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Use a small reversible trial if current evidence is acceptable and review when the reference arrives$qb$, $qb$Assess how much the missing reference could change the decision$qb$, $qb$Delay the trial until every check is complete$qb$, $qb$Choose the cheapest supplier immediately$qb$, $qb$Choose the supplier a colleague prefers$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must decide whether to run a small test today, but one minor cost estimate is uncertain. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Set a safe cost limit, run a reversible test, and update the decision when the estimate is known$qb$, $qb$Assess the highest reasonable cost before starting$qb$, $qb$Wait until the exact cost is confirmed$qb$, $qb$Use the lowest possible cost estimate$qb$, $qb$Cancel the test because information is incomplete$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A small purchase must be made today, but two suppliers have similar offers and one detail is unknown. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose using the important known criteria and keep the decision easy to adjust if the missing detail matters$qb$, $qb$Identify whether the missing detail could change the preferred supplier$qb$, $qb$Delay the purchase until every detail is known$qb$, $qb$Choose the supplier you used last time$qb$, $qb$Choose the cheapest supplier without further review$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to choose whether to start a small task now or tomorrow, but tomorrow's workload is uncertain. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Use current priorities and the cost of delay to make a reasonable choice, then adjust if conditions change$qb$, $qb$Start the task now if doing so protects tomorrow's flexibility$qb$, $qb$Wait until tomorrow's workload is known$qb$, $qb$Always start early regardless of priority$qb$, $qb$Ask a coworker to choose$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must select a meeting tool today, and two options meet the basic need but one feature is still being tested. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose based on essential needs and keep the choice easy to revise if the untested feature matters$qb$, $qb$Compare the importance of the untested feature before deciding$qb$, $qb$Wait until the feature test is complete$qb$, $qb$Choose the tool with more features$qb$, $qb$Choose whichever tool your colleague likes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How should a project lead make operational decisions when choosing between delivery options with incomplete data?$qb$, $qb$Mitigating downside risks by evaluating potential client impact, selecting reliable options, and setting checks.$qb$, $qb$Selecting the fastest theoretical option to maximize speed potential, monitoring progress along the way.$qb$, $qb$Delaying operational choices for several hours to wait for updated background data reports.$qb$, $qb$Asking the client to evaluate risk trade-offs and choose their preferred delivery pathway.$qb$, $qb$Splitting resources equally across available delivery options to hedge operational risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must decide whether to enter a market before a competitor. Demand evidence is promising, regulation is still evolving, and delay may forfeit first-mover advantage. What is the strongest approach?$qb$, $qb$Define the uncertainties that matter most, cap irreversible exposure, stage the commitment, and predefine conditions for scaling, pausing, or exiting.$qb$, $qb$Enter at full scale because early advantage is difficult to recover later.$qb$, $qb$Wait until regulation and demand are both sufficiently clear.$qb$, $qb$Run a small pilot but avoid setting exit criteria until more is learned.$qb$, $qb$Follow the competitor's decision once their market response becomes visible.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must choose between a partnership with proven economics but low future flexibility and a less-proven option preserving access to a potentially dominant ecosystem. What method is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Compare expected value, option value, reversibility and failure exposure; identify evidence that could change the choice and who owns residual risk.$qb$, $qb$Choose proven economics because demonstrated returns should dominate.$qb$, $qb$Choose flexibility because future adaptability matters most.$qb$, $qb$Split investment equally to benefit from both possibilities.$qb$, $qb$Delay until one option is clearly superior on every major dimension.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must decide whether to exit a declining market. Current economics are weak, but a pending regulation could make your capabilities unusually valuable. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Compare exit, stay and option-preserving choices using probability, timing, reversibility and downside exposure, with signals for further commitment.$qb$, $qb$Resolve the immediate pressure and document the remaining risk for later review.$qb$, $qb$Use a narrower solution that improves one dimension but leaves key dependencies unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Transfer the decision to another function without clarifying enterprise ownership.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current plan even when evidence shows its assumptions are weakening.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must commit capital before a volatile market window closes, and several critical facts will remain unknowable. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Separate knowable from irreducible uncertainty, test what can be tested, price downside and reversibility, and make residual-risk ownership explicit.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to choose a meeting date before one person's availability is confirmed. Waiting for certainty also has a cost. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Choose the best provisional date, state the assumption, and adjust if needed$qb$, $qb$Hold the most likely date and confirm it when the person replies$qb$, $qb$Wait to schedule anything until everyone confirms$qb$, $qb$Pick a date without telling anyone it may change$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to choose so you are not responsible$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer complaint keeps returning after quick fixes. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Look for the root cause before choosing another fix$qb$, $qb$Compare the recent complaints for a common pattern$qb$, $qb$Apply the quickest fix again while collecting more information$qb$, $qb$Ask a more experienced colleague for their preferred solution$qb$, $qb$Treat each complaint separately because the details differ$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Customers keep asking the same question even though the answer is in the help guide. What should you investigate first?$qb$, $qb$Why customers are not finding or understanding the answer, then improve the point where the gap occurs.$qb$, $qb$Review recent questions to see where customers look for help.$qb$, $qb$Tell support staff to send the same guide link more often.$qb$, $qb$Add more text to the existing guide without checking the problem.$qb$, $qb$Assume customers are not reading carefully enough.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team keeps missing the same information during handovers. What is the best first improvement?$qb$, $qb$Find why the information is being missed and fix that point in the handoff$qb$, $qb$Review several missed handovers for a common pattern$qb$, $qb$Add another reminder to the current process$qb$, $qb$Ask people to be more careful$qb$, $qb$Accept that handovers sometimes fail$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A task is delayed every Monday but usually runs on time later in the week. What should you investigate first?$qb$, $qb$What is different about Monday inputs, workload, or dependencies$qb$, $qb$Whether the Monday delay follows the same pattern each week$qb$, $qb$Whether the task deadline can move to Tuesday$qb$, $qb$Which person works slowest on Monday$qb$, $qb$How to add more people every Monday$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team fixes the same data error every week. What is the best first step toward a lasting solution?$qb$, $qb$Trace where the error first enters the process and why$qb$, $qb$Review several cases to confirm the same source$qb$, $qb$Create a faster correction method$qb$, $qb$Assign one person to fix the error each week$qb$, $qb$Accept the error as part of the process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer form is often returned because one field is left empty. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Find out why users miss the field before choosing a fix$qb$, $qb$Review examples and ask users what confused them$qb$, $qb$Make the field mandatory immediately$qb$, $qb$Add more instructions to the whole form$qb$, $qb$Blame users for not reading carefully$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process has become slower, but no single step appears much slower than before. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure the full flow and look for small delays, waiting, or repeated handoffs that add up$qb$, $qb$Compare current timing with earlier timing at each step$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the longest step$qb$, $qb$Ask people which step feels slowest$qb$, $qb$Add more staff to the process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team receives many questions about the same instruction. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Identify exactly which part people misunderstand and why before rewriting it$qb$, $qb$Review the repeated questions and find the common point$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the whole instruction immediately$qb$, $qb$Tell people to read more carefully$qb$, $qb$Add more text to every section$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is the most effective approach to identify root causes when service satisfaction scores decline?$qb$, $qb$Deconstructing ticket lifecycle metrics, mapping processing stages, and testing targeted workflow fixes.$qb$, $qb$Holding brainstorming sessions with service agents to gather qualitative opinions on service delays.$qb$, $qb$Establishing daily ticket processing quotas for agents to raise overall operational speed.$qb$, $qb$Escalating satisfaction drops to leadership with recommendations for temporary staffing additions.$qb$, $qb$Sending follow-up survey forms to dissatisfied customers to collect additional individual complaints.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process has a high rework rate, but teams disagree whether the cause is training, system design, or unclear policy. What should happen before choosing a solution?$qb$, $qb$Trace where rework originates, segment the failure patterns, test competing hypotheses, and target the dominant causes.$qb$, $qb$Run refresher training because it is the quickest intervention to test.$qb$, $qb$Upgrade the system because repeated rework usually indicates poor tooling.$qb$, $qb$Ask managers to vote on which cause appears most likely.$qb$, $qb$Introduce stricter quality checks at the end of the process.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A company repeatedly fixes late deliveries by adding expediting staff, yet delays return whenever volume rises. What should leadership investigate?$qb$, $qb$Examine constraints, variability, incentives and flow relationships that create recurring delay across the end-to-end system.$qb$, $qb$Act on the strongest available signal while keeping a later review point.$qb$, $qb$Seek broader consensus before making a limited decision.$qb$, $qb$Use a familiar precedent even though important conditions differ.$qb$, $qb$Choose the easiest path to implement regardless of longer-term consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Complaints rise after a policy change, but service teams use manual exceptions to keep service metrics stable. What should leadership investigate?$qb$, $qb$Trace the causal chain, hidden work and incentives to determine whether the policy creates systemic demand that headline metrics conceal.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Repeated quality failures occur only when demand spikes, although normal-period processes pass every audit. What should leadership investigate?$qb$, $qb$Examine how capacity, queues, incentives, handoffs and controls behave under peak conditions to identify the mechanism that audits of normal operation miss.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A recurring operational failure appears in different departments each quarter, so each incident review names a different local cause. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Look across incidents for common upstream conditions, incentives, interfaces or constraints that migrate the failure rather than treating each location as independent.$qb$, $qb$In this case, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$At first, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A report is often late because information arrives from several people at different times. Previous quick fixes have not stopped recurrence. What should you examine first?$qb$, $qb$Where the flow breaks and why the needed information arrives late$qb$, $qb$Which contributor is late most often$qb$, $qb$Whether a reminder email would solve the problem$qb$, $qb$Whether the report deadline can be moved$qb$, $qb$Who should be blamed for the delays$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A service issue has been fixed several times but keeps returning in slightly different forms. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Look across the cases for a common underlying cause and redesign the part of the process creating it$qb$, $qb$Compare the recurring cases and identify the common pattern$qb$, $qb$Create a faster standard fix for each new case$qb$, $qb$Assign a specialist to handle all future cases$qb$, $qb$Treat each case separately because the symptoms differ$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Several teams report different symptoms after a common system change. What is the best first approach?$qb$, $qb$Map the symptoms against the shared change and look for a common mechanism or dependency before fixing each symptom separately$qb$, $qb$Compare the incidents and identify when and where each began$qb$, $qb$Fix the most disruptive symptom first$qb$, $qb$Ask each team to solve its own issue$qb$, $qb$Roll back the system change immediately$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-127$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A report is produced every week because leadership requested it years ago. Current leaders rarely reference it, but preparing it takes several hours. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the decision or control the report is supposed to support today, verify whether that need still exists, and redesign or stop the report accordingly.$qb$, $qb$Ask recipients whether they still read the report.$qb$, $qb$Automate the report so preparation takes less time.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the report to half its current length.$qb$, $qb$Continue it because leadership originally requested it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-171$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A task is said to require three days because it has always been completed through the same sequence of steps. You are asked whether the time can be reduced safely. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Break the task into required outcomes, real constraints, dependencies, and assumptions, then rebuild the process from what is actually necessary.$qb$, $qb$Identify the slowest current steps and look for ways to shorten each of them.$qb$, $qb$Copy a faster process used by another team and adapt it to your work.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the time allowed for every existing step by the same percentage.$qb$, $qb$Accept three days as the proven requirement because repeated experience is strong evidence.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-217$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A rule requires every request to receive two approvals, even though many requests are routine and low value. You are asked whether the process can be improved safely. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the risk each approval is meant to control, determine which request characteristics actually create that risk, and test a proportionate approval path for lower-risk requests.$qb$, $qb$Remove one approval from all requests and monitor whether problems increase.$qb$, $qb$Keep two approvals but ask approvers to respond faster.$qb$, $qb$Copy a one-approval process used by another department.$qb$, $qb$Keep the rule because two approvals are inherently safer than one.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2379$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A paper-era process still requires initials on every page, but the new system stores one locked, version-controlled document. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm page initials serve no separate requirement and remove them if digital integrity fully meets the original purpose.$qb$, $qb$Pre-fill the field automatically from system metadata while allowing optional manual override.$qb$, $qb$Keep requiring manual entry because it forces employees to review each page carefully.$qb$, $qb$Remove the field without verifying if downstream audit processes require it.$qb$, $qb$Add an extra supervisor sign-off step to confirm that manual entries match system timestamps.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2829$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process requires two managers to approve a low-risk request because years ago neither manager had full authority alone. Current policy now gives either one authority. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the current authority rule and remove redundant dual approval for cases it covers, retaining additional review only where another control requires it.$qb$, $qb$Keep both approvals because more control is safer.$qb$, $qb$Remove all approvals for low-risk requests.$qb$, $qb$Let employees choose whether to get one or two approvals.$qb$, $qb$Alternate which manager approves each week.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-355$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team requires a manager signature on every routine update because paper forms once needed physical authorization. Updates are now digital and fully logged. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify what assurance the signature was meant to provide, determine whether the digital audit trail and decision rights already satisfy it, and redesign approval only if the control objective remains protected.$qb$, $qb$Replace the signature with an email approval from the manager.$qb$, $qb$Remove manager approval from every update because the system is digital.$qb$, $qb$Keep the signature requirement but use electronic signatures.$qb$, $qb$Keep the process unchanged because manager authorization is an important control.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-493$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team asks every customer for the same ten pieces of information because the form was designed for the most complex case. Most cases need only four. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which information is necessary for each decision or risk level, then redesign the form so common cases ask only what is needed and complex cases trigger additional questions.$qb$, $qb$Remove the six least-used questions for everyone.$qb$, $qb$Keep all ten questions because standard forms are easier to manage.$qb$, $qb$Create two separate forms, one short and one long, and let customers choose.$qb$, $qb$Tell customers to leave irrelevant fields blank.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-539$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer request requires three internal handoffs because different departments historically owned different pieces. Technology now allows one team to complete all three steps. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which ownership, control or expertise requirements still justify separate handoffs, then redesign the flow around the minimum necessary boundaries.$qb$, $qb$Move all three steps to one team immediately because technology now permits it.$qb$, $qb$Keep the handoffs but automate the transfer between teams.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the three handoffs to two as a compromise.$qb$, $qb$Keep the structure because departmental ownership should not change solely due to technology.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-631$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team requires a weekly spreadsheet because a former system could not produce the information directly. The current system can now generate the same data automatically. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm whether the spreadsheet provides any additional decision, control or transformation value, and retire it if the automated system output fully meets the underlying need.$qb$, $qb$Keep the spreadsheet but populate it automatically from the system.$qb$, $qb$Stop the spreadsheet immediately because automation is available.$qb$, $qb$Continue it as a backup in case the system report fails.$qb$, $qb$Ask users whether they prefer the spreadsheet or system report.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-677$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A monthly report includes several charts because they were requested by a leader who left two years ago. Current users rarely discuss them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which decisions each chart is meant to support today, remove or redesign charts with no current purpose, and retain any required historical or control information separately.$qb$, $qb$Remove every chart that has not been discussed recently.$qb$, $qb$Keep all charts because historical continuity is valuable.$qb$, $qb$Move unused charts to an appendix but continue producing them.$qb$, $qb$Ask users to vote on which charts they like.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-723$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process requires customers to sign two separate forms containing almost identical declarations because two departments historically created their own forms. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the legal and operational purpose of each declaration, determine whether one combined declaration can satisfy both, and redesign only after confirming the underlying requirements.$qb$, $qb$Combine the forms immediately because the wording is almost identical.$qb$, $qb$Keep both forms because each department owns one.$qb$, $qb$Ask customers to sign only the first form and copy the signature to the second.$qb$, $qb$Digitize both forms without changing their content.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-769$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team asks for three signatures on a routine document because each manager historically wanted visibility. The digital system already records who reviewed and accepted it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify what authority or assurance each signature was meant to provide, compare that need with the digital record, and remove redundant signatures only where the underlying control is already satisfied.$qb$, $qb$Remove all signatures because the system records reviews.$qb$, $qb$Keep all signatures because managers still need visibility.$qb$, $qb$Reduce three signatures to one as a compromise.$qb$, $qb$Replace handwritten signatures with electronic signatures.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-815$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team creates a separate monthly summary from a dashboard because leaders once could not access the dashboard. Leaders now have full access and use it regularly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Determine whether the summary adds interpretation or decisions beyond the dashboard, and retire or redesign it if its original access purpose no longer exists.$qb$, $qb$Stop the summary immediately because leaders can see the dashboard.$qb$, $qb$Keep the summary because leaders may still appreciate convenience.$qb$, $qb$Automate the summary but keep the same content.$qb$, $qb$Ask leaders whether they like receiving it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-861$qb$, $qb$A2-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A department requires managers to attend every routine review because years ago only managers could access the underlying data. Access is now available to the whole team. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify what decision or authority still requires a manager, and remove mandatory attendance where data access was the only reason while retaining manager involvement for genuine decisions.$qb$, $qb$Stop manager attendance immediately because everyone has data access.$qb$, $qb$Keep managers because their presence improves discipline.$qb$, $qb$Let managers decide individually whether to attend.$qb$, $qb$Replace managers with senior employees at every review.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-082$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You must brief a director using three reports: one shows rising demand, one shows falling conversion, and one shows higher customer complaints. Each uses a different period. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Align the time periods and definitions, identify what can validly be compared, surface the combined signal and uncertainty, and focus the brief on the decision the director needs to make.$qb$, $qb$Summarize each report separately and let the director connect the findings.$qb$, $qb$Use the newest report because it is most current.$qb$, $qb$Average the trends into one overall percentage.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the report with the largest dataset.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1138$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Customer revenue is stable, but one large customer increased spending while many smaller customers reduced spending. A leader asks whether the customer base is healthy. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show the stable total alongside concentration and segment movements, explain the dependency on the large customer, and assess the broader customer trend separately.$qb$, $qb$Say the customer base is healthy because revenue is stable.$qb$, $qb$Say it is unhealthy because smaller customers declined.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the large customer's growth.$qb$, $qb$Average spending changes across all customers.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1184$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A report shows total support cost fell, but the number of customers served also fell significantly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare cost per customer, service level and workload mix before concluding that support became more efficient.$qb$, $qb$Report the lower total cost as efficiency improvement.$qb$, $qb$Say efficiency worsened because fewer customers were served.$qb$, $qb$Ask support staff whether work feels easier.$qb$, $qb$Use only total cost because that is what the budget measures.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1276$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team reports that average project margin improved, but several low-margin projects were cancelled before completion and excluded from the average. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine how cancelled projects are treated, compare a consistent project population, and distinguish genuine margin improvement from selection effects.$qb$, $qb$Report the higher average margin as success.$qb$, $qb$Include every cancelled project at zero margin.$qb$, $qb$Ignore cancellations because they were not completed projects.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance whether the average looks reasonable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-128$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You receive survey results, operational metrics, and five stakeholder opinions about a service problem. The evidence does not fully agree. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate source types and quality, identify converging and conflicting signals, quantify what is known, and present the decision-relevant uncertainty rather than forcing false agreement.$qb$, $qb$Average all numerical evidence and summarize the opinions.$qb$, $qb$Use the stakeholder view from the most senior person.$qb$, $qb$Choose the data source with the largest sample.$qb$, $qb$Present every source in full without prioritizing what matters.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1322$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A product reports higher average order value, but the minimum order size was increased during the same period. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate the mechanical effect of the new minimum from changes in customer purchasing behavior before claiming that customers are spending more voluntarily.$qb$, $qb$Report the higher average order value as growth.$qb$, $qb$Say the change is entirely due to the minimum order.$qb$, $qb$Compare only total revenue.$qb$, $qb$Ask customers whether they noticed the minimum change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1460$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Average delivery time improved after very complex orders were moved into a separate reporting category. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recombine or normalize the reporting categories and compare equivalent orders before concluding that delivery performance improved.$qb$, $qb$Accept the improvement because the official category changed.$qb$, $qb$Ignore complex orders because they are now separate.$qb$, $qb$Compare only routine orders.$qb$, $qb$Ask customers whether deliveries feel faster.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1506$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A department reports higher output per employee after outsourcing a portion of routine work, but outsourced effort is excluded from the calculation. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Include or account for outsourced capacity and comparable workload before concluding that total productivity improved.$qb$, $qb$Accept the higher output per employee because internal staff are more productive.$qb$, $qb$Say productivity declined because work was outsourced.$qb$, $qb$Compare only payroll cost.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees whether they feel more productive.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-172$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have five minutes to brief your manager from a long email thread containing verified facts, repeated opinions, and one unresolved disagreement. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Extract the decision needed, verified facts, material disagreement, risks, and recommended next step while removing repetition and unsupported commentary.$qb$, $qb$Summarize the main positions and attach the full thread for reference.$qb$, $qb$Summarize the messages in chronological order so the manager can see how the discussion developed.$qb$, $qb$Use the latest message as the current position because it is most recent.$qb$, $qb$Give only your preferred conclusion so the manager is not overloaded with detail.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-218$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You receive a customer survey, service data, and sales feedback that point in different directions about the same product issue. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare what each source actually measures, reconcile differences where possible, separate consistent signals from contradictions, and brief the decision-maker on the strongest conclusion plus material uncertainty.$qb$, $qb$Summarize each source separately and let the decision-maker compare them.$qb$, $qb$Give greatest weight to the service data because it is quantitative.$qb$, $qb$Average the positive and negative signals into one overall assessment.$qb$, $qb$Use the source that best matches what customers have recently told senior leaders.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2380$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Revenue per employee rises after support work moves to a shared service whose headcount is excluded. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Include or separately show shared-service effort and comparable work scope before concluding labor productivity improved.$qb$, $qb$Implement a practical interim adjustment to address immediate operational needs while monitoring results.$qb$, $qb$Maintain existing procedures temporarily until formal company-wide guidance is published.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the issue to departmental leadership without first gathering supporting data or options.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the operational discrepancy and proceed with business as usual.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2564$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A department reports lower cost after translation work moves to a shared service and its cost disappears from the local metric. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Include or separately disclose shared-service translation cost and comparable workload before concluding total cost improved.$qb$, $qb$Report local departmental cost savings while mentioning the shared service transfer in a footnote.$qb$, $qb$Assume total organization costs fell because local departmental expenditure decreased.$qb$, $qb$Exclude all shared service expenses from financial comparisons to simplify tracking.$qb$, $qb$Celebrate the local cost reduction without analyzing enterprise-wide expenditures.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-264$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You receive ten customer comments about a new feature: three detailed complaints, five brief positive comments, and two unrelated comments. A manager asks for the main message. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate relevant from unrelated comments, consider the depth and nature of positive and negative evidence, and summarize the emerging themes without treating the small sample as representative of all customers.$qb$, $qb$Report that most relevant comments are positive because five are positive and three are negative.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the three detailed complaints because they contain more information.$qb$, $qb$List all ten comments so the manager can judge them directly.$qb$, $qb$Average the positive and negative comments and describe sentiment as slightly positive.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2830$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A business unit reports higher profit, but the increase came mainly from selling an asset rather than normal operations. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate the one-off asset gain from operating profit, quantify both, and explain the underlying recurring performance before drawing a trend conclusion.$qb$, $qb$Report the higher profit as improved performance.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the asset sale because it is legitimate income.$qb$, $qb$Exclude the asset sale entirely from all reporting.$qb$, $qb$Wait until next quarter before discussing profitability.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-310$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Three teams report different numbers for the same KPI because they use different cut-off times. You need one figure for an executive update today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the definitions and cut-off times, choose the figure that matches the executive reporting rule, and explain any material difference rather than blending incomparable values.$qb$, $qb$Use the most recently updated figure.$qb$, $qb$Average the three numbers to avoid favoring one team's data.$qb$, $qb$Use the figure from the team that owns the largest part of the process.$qb$, $qb$Choose the number closest to last week's trend so the update remains consistent.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-494$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have one report showing strong overall performance and another showing serious problems in one small customer segment. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Present the strong overall result together with the segment exception, quantify its size and impact, and explain whether the exception changes the decision or requires targeted action.$qb$, $qb$Lead with overall performance because it represents most customers.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the problem segment because serious issues should receive priority.$qb$, $qb$Average the two reports into one overall score.$qb$, $qb$Choose the report produced by the more authoritative team.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-632$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager receives twenty comments from a pilot. Most are minor preferences, but two identify a potential safety issue. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate preference feedback from risk evidence, investigate the two safety comments urgently for validity and impact, and summarize the overall feedback without allowing frequency alone to determine importance.$qb$, $qb$Report the most common comments because they represent the majority view.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the two safety comments and ignore the rest.$qb$, $qb$Rank all comments by how many people mentioned each one.$qb$, $qb$Send all twenty comments to the manager without interpretation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-770$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager asks whether a new service is performing well. Revenue is ahead of plan, support tickets are high, and renewal data is not yet available. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Present the strong revenue signal, the support burden and missing renewal evidence, explain what each means, and avoid declaring overall success until the outcome picture is sufficiently complete.$qb$, $qb$Say performance is strong because revenue is ahead of plan.$qb$, $qb$Say performance is concerning because support tickets are high.$qb$, $qb$Average the positive and negative indicators into a balanced view.$qb$, $qb$Wait for renewal data and give no current assessment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-862$qb$, $qb$A2-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A product has high satisfaction among active users, but usage has fallen sharply. A leader asks whether customers like the product. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain that satisfaction describes remaining active users while declining usage may indicate a different problem, investigate who stopped using it, and avoid using satisfaction alone as the answer.$qb$, $qb$Say customers like it because satisfaction is high.$qb$, $qb$Say customers dislike it because usage is falling.$qb$, $qb$Average satisfaction and usage into one health score.$qb$, $qb$Wait for more data before saying anything.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-034$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$To speed up customer order dispatch, your team cuts inspection steps. While dispatch speed improves by 20%, return rates due to missing items rise by 15%. What is your reaction?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the systemic trade-off, restore critical quality checks, and redesign dispatch staging to improve speed without compromising accuracy.$qb$, $qb$Add an extra warning label to shipping boxes advising customers to check contents upon arrival.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the faster dispatch speed while creating a dedicated fast-track return queue to handle increased returns.$qb$, $qb$Revert strictly to the original slower dispatch workflow to eliminate order errors completely.$qb$, $qb$Pass return rate metrics to the customer service team to manage post-delivery complaints.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-080$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A new intake rule reduces errors entering your process, but completion time rises because customers submit more follow-up questions. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure the end-to-end effect, identify why the rule creates extra questions, and redesign it so error prevention does not simply shift effort downstream.$qb$, $qb$Keep the rule because intake accuracy improved.$qb$, $qb$Remove the rule because total completion time is worse.$qb$, $qb$Ask customer support to answer the extra questions faster.$qb$, $qb$Add more instructions without checking which part is confusing.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1136$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A call center reduces call length by asking customers to call back for issues needing research. Average handling time improves, but repeat calls rise. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure total customer contacts and resolution time, examine the incentive created by the handling-time target, and redesign the process and metrics around durable resolution.$qb$, $qb$Keep the process because shorter calls improve capacity.$qb$, $qb$Stop measuring handling time entirely.$qb$, $qb$Tell agents not to ask customers to call back unless absolutely necessary.$qb$, $qb$Add more agents to handle the repeat calls.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1182$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A warehouse team meets its picking-speed target by leaving packing checks until the end of the shift, creating a late bottleneck. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure the full order flow, identify how the speed target shifts work downstream, and redesign sequencing and metrics around completed orders rather than picking alone.$qb$, $qb$Keep the picking target and add overtime for packing.$qb$, $qb$Tell pickers to help packing after they finish.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the picking target without analyzing flow.$qb$, $qb$Accept the bottleneck because picking performance is strong.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-126$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team automates a step and saves four hours each week, but another team now spends six extra hours resolving exceptions. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the total process impact, understand why exceptions increased, and redesign the automation or handoff so the net system improves.$qb$, $qb$Keep the automation because your team's productivity target improved.$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team to automate its exception handling.$qb$, $qb$Remove the automation immediately.$qb$, $qb$Transfer ownership of the exceptions to a third team.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1274$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team improves its response SLA by sending quick acknowledgements, but actual resolution time is unchanged and customer satisfaction falls. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate acknowledgement from resolution, review the metric incentive, and redesign service measures and workflow around meaningful progress and final resolution.$qb$, $qb$Keep the SLA because fast acknowledgement is valuable.$qb$, $qb$Stop acknowledgements completely.$qb$, $qb$Tell customers that resolution naturally takes longer.$qb$, $qb$Add a satisfaction target without changing the workflow.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1320$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team improves its on-time metric by changing promised dates shortly before they are due. Customers still receive work at the same actual time. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure performance against the original customer commitment and analyze date changes, then redesign the metric and controls so schedule performance cannot improve through rebaselining alone.$qb$, $qb$Keep the metric because revised dates are official dates.$qb$, $qb$Stop allowing any date changes.$qb$, $qb$Ask teams to change dates earlier.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the issue if customers are not complaining.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1412$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team improves its completion metric by splitting unfinished cases into several smaller cases and closing the easy parts. Customer issues remain unresolved. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure resolution at the customer-issue level, examine the incentive created by the metric, and redesign completion rules so splitting work cannot create false success.$qb$, $qb$Keep the metric because the smaller cases are genuinely completed.$qb$, $qb$Ban splitting cases under any circumstances.$qb$, $qb$Add a separate customer-satisfaction metric but keep the process.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to use fewer subcases.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1458$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team meets its closure target by closing cases when work leaves their queue, even though another team still must finish them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure completion at the end-to-end customer outcome, not queue exit, and change the closure rule so work cannot be counted complete while downstream action remains.$qb$, $qb$Keep the rule because the team's work is finished.$qb$, $qb$Ban all transfers to other teams.$qb$, $qb$Add a second metric but leave closure unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Ask downstream teams to close faster.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1504$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team improves its 'first-time completion' metric by returning difficult requests to customers for minor clarifications instead of working them through. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine the return behavior and customer effort, define completion around genuine resolution, and adjust the metric so avoidable returns do not create better performance.$qb$, $qb$Keep the metric because returned requests are incomplete inputs.$qb$, $qb$Ban all requests for clarification.$qb$, $qb$Add a customer-effort survey but keep the workflow.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to return fewer cases.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1550$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team improves its backlog metric by moving difficult items into a separate 'special review' queue that is not counted. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure the full unresolved workload, define when special review is legitimate, and change reporting so moving an item cannot make backlog disappear.$qb$, $qb$Keep the separate queue because those items are genuinely different.$qb$, $qb$Ban special review entirely.$qb$, $qb$Add a note explaining the excluded queue.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to move fewer items.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-170$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team speeds up its processing time by skipping a review step, but another team now spends more time correcting the resulting errors. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure the end-to-end effect on total effort and turnaround time, then redesign the workflow so local speed does not create greater downstream cost.$qb$, $qb$Restore the review step and monitor whether total turnaround time improves.$qb$, $qb$Keep the faster process but ask the downstream team to improve how it handles corrections.$qb$, $qb$Track only the errors that cause major rework and ignore small corrections.$qb$, $qb$Keep the change because your team's processing target has improved.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-308$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A quality team reduces defects by adding more checks, but delivery time grows and teams begin bypassing the checks on urgent work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine where defects originate, which checks add real control, and why urgent work bypasses them, then redesign the system to control risk without creating an unusable path.$qb$, $qb$Keep all checks and enforce them more strictly on urgent work.$qb$, $qb$Remove the newest checks until delivery time improves.$qb$, $qb$Create a separate fast path for urgent work with no checks.$qb$, $qb$Accept longer delivery because quality is more important than speed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-446$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A company shortens approval time by allowing more people to approve requests. Approval is faster, but inconsistent decisions increase. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine decision criteria, training, authority and feedback across approvers, then redesign the system so added capacity does not reduce consistency.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the number of approvers back to the original group.$qb$, $qb$Keep all approvers but require managers to review inconsistent decisions afterward.$qb$, $qb$Publish more examples and assume consistency will improve.$qb$, $qb$Accept some inconsistency because faster approval is the main objective.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-492$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A company introduces a bonus for rapid issue closure. Closure time improves, but customer complaints about repeat problems increase. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine how the bonus is shaping behavior, include durable resolution and repeat-issue measures, and redesign the incentive so fast closure is valuable only when the problem stays solved.$qb$, $qb$Keep the bonus but tell staff not to close issues prematurely.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the bonus amount so staff feel less pressure.$qb$, $qb$Remove the bonus and return to the old system.$qb$, $qb$Accept more repeat issues because customers still receive faster initial closure.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-630$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team reduces waiting time by keeping more work in progress at once. Individual tasks start sooner, but total completion time begins to increase. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure queue size, flow and completion time, identify whether excess work in progress is causing congestion, and set limits or sequencing rules that optimize total flow.$qb$, $qb$Keep starting work quickly because visible waiting time has improved.$qb$, $qb$Tell employees to work faster on each open task.$qb$, $qb$Stop accepting new work until every current task is finished.$qb$, $qb$Add more status meetings to track all open work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-676$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team reduces customer waiting by moving unfinished cases to another queue at the end of each day. The first queue looks better, but total unresolved cases rise. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure cases across the full lifecycle, remove incentives to shift unfinished work between queues, and redesign ownership and metrics around actual resolution.$qb$, $qb$Ask the second queue to increase capacity.$qb$, $qb$Stop all transfers between queues immediately.$qb$, $qb$Keep the transfer but report both queue sizes together.$qb$, $qb$Accept the process because customers leave the first queue faster.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-722$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A department reduces overtime by postponing unfinished work to the next month. Monthly cost looks better, but backlog steadily grows. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure backlog, service impact and total workload alongside overtime, and redesign capacity or prioritization so cost improvement does not come from shifting work across reporting periods.$qb$, $qb$Keep the overtime reduction and add a backlog target.$qb$, $qb$Allow more overtime until the backlog is cleared.$qb$, $qb$Tell staff to finish more work during normal hours.$qb$, $qb$Accept the backlog because reducing overtime was the department goal.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-814$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team improves first-response time by sending immediate standard replies, but customers increasingly contact the team again because the replies do not solve their questions. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure repeat contact and total resolution time, distinguish where standard replies help, and redesign the response metric and process around useful resolution rather than first-touch speed alone.$qb$, $qb$Keep the standard replies and train customers to provide clearer questions.$qb$, $qb$Remove all standard replies.$qb$, $qb$Add more detail to every standard reply.$qb$, $qb$Accept repeat contact because first-response performance has improved.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-860$qb$, $qb$A2-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team reduces backlog by closing old requests that have had no customer response. Customers later reopen many of them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Measure reopen patterns and customer contact timing, distinguish genuinely abandoned requests from unresolved ones, and redesign closure rules around durable resolution rather than backlog appearance.$qb$, $qb$Keep closing old requests and handle reopened ones as new work.$qb$, $qb$Stop closing any request without explicit customer confirmation.$qb$, $qb$Send more reminders before closure.$qb$, $qb$Accept reopenings because the backlog metric improves.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A vendor proposes a software tool claiming it will increase team productivity by 30%. The proposal includes glowing client testimonials. How do you evaluate this proposal?$qb$, $qb$Analyze raw data sources behind the productivity claim, assess compatibility with existing team workflows, and request a structured trial.$qb$, $qb$Present the proposal to your team lead, highlighting the positive client testimonials and potential efficiency gains.$qb$, $qb$Ask the vendor for a discount on licensing fees before deciding whether to pilot the software tool.$qb$, $qb$Consult with another internal department to see if they have utilized similar vendor tools in the past.$qb$, $qb$Accept the vendor claims as valid industry benchmarks and recommend adopting the tool to department leadership.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-073$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A vendor claims its service cut processing time by 25% for another company. Your team is considering a purchase. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine how the result was measured, the comparison baseline, the other company's context, implementation requirements, and evidence relevant to your own environment before drawing a conclusion.$qb$, $qb$Ask the vendor for references from two more customers.$qb$, $qb$Assume the result is realistic because the vendor published it publicly.$qb$, $qb$Reject the claim because vendor case studies are marketing material.$qb$, $qb$Choose the vendor if the quoted price is lower than your current operating cost.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1267$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager says productivity improved because completed tasks increased, but the team also began counting several small subtasks separately. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Reconcile the measurement definition and compare equivalent units of work before concluding that underlying productivity improved.$qb$, $qb$Accept the increase because more items were completed.$qb$, $qb$Use hours worked instead of task counts.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees whether they feel more productive.$qb$, $qb$Keep the new measure because it shows more detail.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1451$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager says service quality improved because complaints fell, but customer volume also fell sharply. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare complaints relative to customer volume and other service signals before concluding that quality improved.$qb$, $qb$Report fewer complaints as improvement.$qb$, $qb$Say quality worsened because volume fell.$qb$, $qb$Ignore volume because complaints are the official metric.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff whether service feels better.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-163$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says customer complaints rose after a process change and therefore the process change caused the increase. The timing matches, but you have not seen the underlying data. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the complaint trend, definitions, affected groups, other changes in the same period, and plausible alternative causes before concluding causation.$qb$, $qb$Ask for the complaint data and compare the period before and after the process change.$qb$, $qb$Accept the link provisionally because the timing is strong evidence, but monitor another month.$qb$, $qb$Ask several experienced colleagues whether they also think the process change is the cause.$qb$, $qb$Recommend reversing the process immediately because customer complaints are too important to risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-209$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A presentation claims that one branch performs better because its employees are more experienced. The chart shows correlation but no analysis of other differences. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the underlying branch data, experience distribution, workload, customer mix, and other plausible factors before accepting experience as the cause of better performance.$qb$, $qb$Compare average employee experience between the high- and low-performing branches.$qb$, $qb$Accept experience as the likely explanation but state that the chart does not prove causation.$qb$, $qb$Ask branch managers whether experienced employees generally perform better.$qb$, $qb$Use the chart to recommend hiring more experienced employees at other branches.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-255$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager says a training program is successful because employees who attended have higher performance scores than employees who did not. Attendance was voluntary. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check whether attendees differed before training, how performance was measured, and what other factors could explain the difference before attributing the higher scores to the program.$qb$, $qb$Compare the average scores of attendees and non-attendees more carefully.$qb$, $qb$Accept that the program probably helped but avoid claiming it was the only cause.$qb$, $qb$Ask attendees whether they believe the training improved their performance.$qb$, $qb$Recommend expanding the program because the performance difference is already visible.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-439$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team says a new reminder system reduced late submissions because lateness fell after it was introduced. The submission deadline was also moved by two days. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate the effects of the deadline change and reminder system using comparable periods or groups before concluding which change reduced lateness.$qb$, $qb$Compare lateness before and after the reminder system and note the deadline change as a limitation.$qb$, $qb$Assume both changes contributed and keep them both.$qb$, $qb$Ask submitters which change helped them more.$qb$, $qb$Credit the reminder system because it was specifically designed to reduce lateness.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-485$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager says a team is more productive because it completed more tasks this month. The average task size was smaller than last month. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare workload size, complexity, quality and available capacity before concluding that the higher task count represents higher productivity.$qb$, $qb$Report the higher task count but mention that tasks were smaller.$qb$, $qb$Divide task count by team size and use that as productivity.$qb$, $qb$Accept that productivity improved because more work items were completed.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team whether they felt more productive this month.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-577$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager says a process change improved quality because error counts fell. The total number of transactions also fell substantially. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare error rates as well as counts, examine transaction mix and other changes, and only then judge whether underlying quality improved.$qb$, $qb$Report the lower error count but mention lower volume.$qb$, $qb$Assume quality improved because fewer errors reached customers.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees whether the new process feels more accurate.$qb$, $qb$Use the error count because absolute customer errors are what matter most.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-623$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A department reports that employee satisfaction improved after introducing flexible hours. During the same period, several dissatisfied employees left. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine who remained and who left, response rates, comparable groups and other changes before concluding that flexible hours caused the improvement.$qb$, $qb$Report the satisfaction improvement but note that some employees left.$qb$, $qb$Assume flexible hours helped because the remaining employees are more satisfied.$qb$, $qb$Ask current employees whether flexible hours improved their experience.$qb$, $qb$Recommend expanding flexible hours because the survey moved positively.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-669$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A dashboard shows that one team has fewer errors than another, but the teams handle different types of work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare error rates within comparable work types, complexity and volume before concluding that one team performs better.$qb$, $qb$Report the lower-error team as the better performer but note that work differs.$qb$, $qb$Compare total errors because customers experience errors regardless of work type.$qb$, $qb$Ask managers which team's work is harder.$qb$, $qb$Use the dashboard ranking because the metric was designed for comparison.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-715$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A report shows that customers who use a certain feature stay longer. A colleague concludes the feature causes retention. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check whether longer-term customers are simply more likely to discover the feature, compare similar customer groups and timing, and test alternative explanations before claiming causation.$qb$, $qb$Accept that the feature probably improves retention but describe the evidence as correlational.$qb$, $qb$Compare retention of feature users and non-users in more detail.$qb$, $qb$Ask customers who use the feature whether it makes them stay.$qb$, $qb$Recommend promoting the feature because the retention association is strong.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-761$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager says customer satisfaction is improving because the latest survey score is higher. The latest survey had far fewer responses than usual. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check response count, respondent mix, confidence or variability, and comparable prior surveys before concluding the higher score represents a real improvement.$qb$, $qb$Report the higher score but note the smaller sample.$qb$, $qb$Assume the score improved because respondents had no reason to answer differently.$qb$, $qb$Combine the latest score with the previous score to smooth the result.$qb$, $qb$Use the latest score because it is the most current evidence.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-807$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team reports that a new workflow increased output by 20%, but staffing also increased by 25%. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare output per unit of capacity, quality and workload mix before concluding that the workflow itself improved productivity.$qb$, $qb$Report the 20% output increase and note the staffing increase.$qb$, $qb$Say productivity declined because staffing grew faster than output.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees whether the new workflow feels faster.$qb$, $qb$Keep the workflow because total output is higher.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-853$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager says a campaign worked because sales rose during the campaign week. A major competitor was also out of stock that week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine comparable periods, customer sources and the competitor effect before attributing the sales increase to the campaign.$qb$, $qb$Report the sales increase but mention the competitor shortage.$qb$, $qb$Assume both factors helped and call the campaign successful.$qb$, $qb$Ask customers whether they noticed the campaign.$qb$, $qb$Repeat the campaign because sales clearly improved.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You must choose between two delivery routes for a time-sensitive freight shipment. Data on weather delays for Route A is incomplete, while Route B is longer but stable. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess available data, evaluate downside risk impact on client deadlines, choose the reliable route, and set up contingency tracking checkpoints.$qb$, $qb$Select the shorter Route A to maximize speed potential, establishing continuous status checks along the way.$qb$, $qb$Delay the dispatch decision for two hours to wait for updated weather reports on Route A.$qb$, $qb$Ask the client to choose between a faster route with risk versus a longer guaranteed route.$qb$, $qb$Split the shipment equally between both routes to hedge delivery risk across options.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1131$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may require weekend support for a launch. Reserving specialist cover now has a fee, but waiting could leave no one available. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate the likelihood and business impact of weekend need, compare it with the reservation fee, and secure proportionate cover if preserving availability is worth the cost.$qb$, $qb$Reserve the full team immediately.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the customer confirms.$qb$, $qb$Ask specialists to remain informally available without booking them.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer weekend support cannot be guaranteed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1177$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may postpone a launch. You can cancel a specialist booking today for free, but after today there is a fee; keeping the booking protects the original date. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the probability and impact of postponement versus losing specialist availability, and decide whether the option value justifies keeping the booking past the free-cancellation point.$qb$, $qb$Cancel now because it is still free.$qb$, $qb$Keep the booking because the original date is still official.$qb$, $qb$Ask the specialist to remain available without a booking.$qb$, $qb$Let the customer decide whether you should pay the future fee.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1269$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A venue for a training event is confirmed, but there is a moderate chance of transport disruption that day. A nearby backup can be held until next week at low cost. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess disruption likelihood and attendee impact, compare it with the hold cost, and preserve the backup if the option value is worthwhile until the risk becomes clearer.$qb$, $qb$Switch to the backup now.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the risk because the main venue is confirmed.$qb$, $qb$Book both venues fully.$qb$, $qb$Ask attendees to decide which venue should be used.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1407$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may bring a launch forward by one week. Reserving specialist support for both dates costs a modest extra fee. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the likelihood and impact of the earlier launch, compare the extra reservation cost with losing support, and preserve both dates if the option value warrants it.$qb$, $qb$Reserve both dates automatically.$qb$, $qb$Keep only the current official date.$qb$, $qb$Ask specialists to remain informally free for the earlier date.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer an earlier launch is impossible.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1499$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may need an extra room for an event. Holding it until next week has a small fee; booking later may be impossible. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate the likelihood and impact of needing the room, compare that with the hold fee, and preserve the option if the expected value supports it.$qb$, $qb$Hold the room automatically because availability is uncertain.$qb$, $qb$Do not hold it because the need is unconfirmed.$qb$, $qb$Book the room fully now.$qb$, $qb$Ask the venue to keep it informally without a fee.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-165$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You must choose between two workable approaches today, but important information that could affect the choice will not arrive until next week. Waiting also delays the project. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which unknowns could materially change the decision, compare the cost of waiting with acting, and choose a reversible or risk-limited path with explicit assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that currently looks stronger and review the decision when the missing information arrives.$qb$, $qb$Use the approach that has worked most often in previous projects.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the missing information because making the wrong decision would be difficult to defend.$qb$, $qb$Ask the most experienced colleague to choose so the uncertainty is handled by better judgment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-211$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer wants an answer today, but one technical test that could change your recommendation will finish tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain what is known, how the pending test could change the recommendation, give a conditional answer if useful, and commit to a final update when the test finishes.$qb$, $qb$Give your current best recommendation and mention that one test is still pending.$qb$, $qb$Wait until tomorrow and give no recommendation today.$qb$, $qb$Give a firm answer based on current evidence because the customer asked for a decision today.$qb$, $qb$Ask a senior technical colleague to choose the answer so you are not responsible for the uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-257$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A backup supplier can reserve capacity for you today for a small fee. Your main supplier may still deliver on time, but will not confirm until next week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare the reservation cost with the impact of having no backup, assess the main supplier's risk, and reserve proportionate backup capacity if the downside justifies it.$qb$, $qb$Pay the reservation fee immediately because backup capacity is always safer.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the main supplier's confirmation so you do not spend money unnecessarily.$qb$, $qb$Switch the full order to the backup supplier now.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager whether paying for unused backup capacity would be acceptable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-303$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Weather may disrupt a shipment due tomorrow, but forecasts are still uncertain. An earlier alternative route costs more and must be booked within two hours. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the disruption probability and business impact, compare the alternative-route cost, and book or reserve the alternative if the expected downside justifies acting before certainty.$qb$, $qb$Book the alternative immediately because avoiding delay is safest.$qb$, $qb$Keep the normal route because the disruption is not certain.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the next forecast even if the alternative booking window closes.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer whether they are willing to pay for the alternative before assessing the risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-349$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A component may become scarce next month. Buying extra now costs storage money, but waiting could stop production if the shortage occurs. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate shortage likelihood and production impact, compare carrying cost with disruption cost, and secure a proportionate buffer or option if the risk justifies it.$qb$, $qb$Buy as much extra stock as the budget allows because production stoppage is serious.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the shortage is confirmed so inventory is not wasted.$qb$, $qb$Ask the supplier to guarantee availability without changing your stock level.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance to decide because the choice involves cost risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-395$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A machine shows an unusual warning but is still operating normally. Stopping it now delays production; continuing could worsen a fault if the warning is serious. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the warning's known severity and available diagnostics, assess the consequence of failure versus a controlled stop, and choose a proportionate action with a clear trigger for shutdown.$qb$, $qb$Stop the machine immediately because equipment warnings should never be ignored.$qb$, $qb$Continue production until performance actually changes.$qb$, $qb$Ask the most experienced operator whether they would keep running it.$qb$, $qb$Reset the warning and continue if it does not reappear immediately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-441$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer may double an order next month but will not confirm for two weeks. Reserving production capacity now has a cost. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate the probability and value of the larger order, compare reservation cost with the risk of lost capacity, and reserve a proportionate option if the expected value supports it.$qb$, $qb$Reserve all required capacity now because losing the order would be worse.$qb$, $qb$Wait for confirmation so no reservation cost is wasted.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to pay the reservation cost before you consider holding capacity.$qb$, $qb$Ask production to keep the capacity informally without recording a commitment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-487$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A delivery route may be disrupted by a planned public event, but the final road closures will be announced only tomorrow. Your delivery is early the next morning. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify credible alternative routes now, assess their cost and timing, and set a trigger based on the closure announcement so the delivery can switch without last-minute confusion.$qb$, $qb$Choose the alternative route now to remove all risk.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the final closure announcement before doing any planning.$qb$, $qb$Keep the normal route because closures are not yet confirmed.$qb$, $qb$Ask the driver to decide on the morning based on traffic conditions.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-533$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier offers a one-time chance to buy extra material at a low price. Future demand is uncertain and the material expires after six months. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate credible demand ranges, expiry and holding cost, compare them with the saving and shortage risk, and buy only the quantity justified across those scenarios.$qb$, $qb$Buy the maximum amount because the price may not be available again.$qb$, $qb$Buy nothing because uncertain demand makes extra inventory risky.$qb$, $qb$Split the difference and buy half the offered quantity.$qb$, $qb$Ask the supplier what quantity other customers are buying and follow that benchmark.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-579$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier's quality score has declined slightly for two months, but no major failure has occurred. Switching suppliers would be costly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Investigate the trend and causes, increase proportionate monitoring or corrective action, and define thresholds that would trigger stronger intervention or switching.$qb$, $qb$Switch suppliers now before a major failure occurs.$qb$, $qb$Continue as normal because no major failure has happened.$qb$, $qb$Ask the supplier for reassurance that quality will improve.$qb$, $qb$Reduce orders by half immediately without discussing the trend.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-625$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier says a critical part may arrive one day late, but there is a costly local substitute available today. Production can absorb a one-day delay if nothing else slips. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the probability and downstream impact of the delay, define the latest safe point for buying the substitute, and preserve the option rather than purchasing or rejecting it immediately.$qb$, $qb$Buy the substitute now because the part is critical.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the supplier because production can absorb one day.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the original order and switch fully to the local substitute.$qb$, $qb$Ask production to decide because they will experience the delay.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-671$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A delivery partner warns that fuel disruption may affect tomorrow's route. An alternate partner can be booked now but charges a cancellation fee if unused. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the disruption likelihood and delivery impact, compare the cancellation fee with failure cost, and reserve the alternate if the option value justifies it.$qb$, $qb$Book the alternate immediately because the delivery must be protected.$qb$, $qb$Wait until disruption is confirmed to avoid cancellation cost.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the original partner and switch fully to the alternate.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer whether they will pay the cancellation fee.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-717$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A warehouse may need extra temporary space next month. Booking now secures a good rate, but final demand will not be known for two weeks. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate demand ranges and overflow impact, compare reservation or cancellation cost, and secure a proportionate option if losing space would be more costly.$qb$, $qb$Book all the extra space now because rates may rise.$qb$, $qb$Wait for final demand so no unused space is paid for.$qb$, $qb$Book half the estimated space as a compromise.$qb$, $qb$Ask the warehouse provider what they recommend.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-763$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A maintenance contractor may not be available on your preferred date next month. Reserving a backup now costs a small non-refundable fee. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess how critical the maintenance date is, the probability and impact of losing the primary contractor, and reserve backup capacity if the option cost is justified.$qb$, $qb$Reserve the backup immediately because the fee is small.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the primary contractor confirms.$qb$, $qb$Book only the backup and cancel the primary request.$qb$, $qb$Ask the primary contractor to guarantee availability.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-809$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A shipment may arrive late, and you can pay a modest fee now to reserve weekend unloading capacity. If the shipment is on time, the reservation will be unused. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the lateness probability and cost of losing another day, compare it with the reservation fee, and reserve capacity if preserving the option has sufficient value.$qb$, $qb$Reserve the weekend capacity because the fee is modest.$qb$, $qb$Do not reserve because the shipment may arrive on time.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the shipment is confirmed late even if capacity may be gone.$qb$, $qb$Ask the carrier to pay the reservation fee.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-120$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An approval queue is repeatedly delayed. Everyone assumes the approvers are too slow, but the delays vary widely by request type. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Analyze queue stages and request types to locate where waiting actually occurs, then address the specific cause rather than assuming approver speed is the problem.$qb$, $qb$Ask approvers to respond faster.$qb$, $qb$Add more approvers to the queue.$qb$, $qb$Escalate every request older than two days.$qb$, $qb$Increase the official turnaround time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1268$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Customers often select the wrong delivery option because the cheapest option appears first even when it cannot meet their requested date. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use customer date requirements to guide or filter valid choices, clarify the trade-off at selection, and test whether wrong selections and later corrections fall.$qb$, $qb$Move the fastest option to the top for everyone.$qb$, $qb$Add more text describing every delivery option.$qb$, $qb$Let support correct the option after ordering.$qb$, $qb$Remove the cheapest option entirely.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1498$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Customers repeatedly call after submitting an online request because the confirmation page does not show what happens next. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the uncertainty driving calls and test a confirmation that states receipt, next step and expected timing, then measure whether follow-up calls fall.$qb$, $qb$Tell customers not to call until the service deadline passes.$qb$, $qb$Add more staff to answer confirmation calls.$qb$, $qb$Send a longer instruction guide before submission.$qb$, $qb$Remove the confirmation page.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-164$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team misses a weekly cutoff by a few minutes almost every week. The first suggestion is to add more reminders. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Trace where time is actually lost across the workflow, identify the recurring constraint, and test a change aimed at that cause.$qb$, $qb$Review the last few misses, identify the most likely cause, and try a focused improvement.$qb$, $qb$Add automated reminders because they are quick to implement and may improve timeliness.$qb$, $qb$Tell the slowest contributor to submit earlier so the rest of the process has more time.$qb$, $qb$Move the cutoff later so the team can meet it consistently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-210$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team keeps adding people to a manual checking process, yet errors continue at roughly the same rate. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Analyze where errors enter the process and why they survive the checks, then test a change aimed at the failure point instead of adding more checkers.$qb$, $qb$Review recent errors and give the checking team additional instructions.$qb$, $qb$Add another checker because more review should eventually reduce errors.$qb$, $qb$Make the existing checkers accountable for every error that escapes.$qb$, $qb$Accept the error rate because additional checking has already been tried.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-256$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A service team keeps missing a response target. Adding reminders and daily follow-ups has not improved the result. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Map the work from request arrival to response, identify where requests wait or loop, and test a change aimed at the actual bottleneck.$qb$, $qb$Increase the number of reminders and make them more visible.$qb$, $qb$Ask the slowest team members to explain why they miss the target.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the response target temporarily so the team can rebuild confidence.$qb$, $qb$Accept that the target may be unrealistic because repeated follow-up has not worked.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-348$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring approval is often delayed because requests arrive with missing attachments. Staff already receive a checklist before submitting. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Investigate why attachments are still missed, then test a submission control or workflow cue that detects the missing item before the request enters approval.$qb$, $qb$Send the checklist again whenever a request is incomplete.$qb$, $qb$Add more detail to the checklist about each attachment.$qb$, $qb$Reject incomplete requests without explanation so submitters learn the requirement.$qb$, $qb$Add another approver to catch missing attachments earlier.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-394$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A shared mailbox receives repeated questions that are answered in an automated reply, but people continue sending follow-up emails. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the follow-up questions to see what the automated reply fails to resolve, then test changes to the message or process that address the actual remaining need.$qb$, $qb$Make the automated reply longer and include more information.$qb$, $qb$Send the automated reply a second time when people follow up.$qb$, $qb$Tell users that their question was already answered automatically.$qb$, $qb$Assign more staff to handle the follow-up emails.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-440$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team receives many incomplete internal requests even though every field on the request form is mandatory. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review which information is incomplete and how users are entering it, then test validation, examples, or workflow changes that improve information quality rather than merely field completion.$qb$, $qb$Add more mandatory fields so users provide more detail.$qb$, $qb$Reject incomplete requests and tell users to try again.$qb$, $qb$Send a detailed guide explaining what good answers look like.$qb$, $qb$Assign someone to correct incomplete requests before they reach the team.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-486$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A helpdesk receives many password-reset requests even though users have a self-service reset tool. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Analyze why users bypass or fail with self-service, observe the failure points, and test changes to access, guidance or the reset flow before adding helpdesk capacity.$qb$, $qb$Send users more reminders about the self-service tool.$qb$, $qb$Tell helpdesk staff to redirect every caller to self-service.$qb$, $qb$Add a chatbot that repeats the reset instructions.$qb$, $qb$Accept the requests because some users prefer human help.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-532$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees often submit duplicate requests because they cannot tell whether their first request was received. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Investigate the visibility gap, test an acknowledgement and status mechanism, and measure whether duplicate submissions fall.$qb$, $qb$Tell employees not to submit a second request within 24 hours.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to delete duplicate requests when they see them.$qb$, $qb$Add a warning on the form saying duplicates slow processing.$qb$, $qb$Increase capacity so duplicate requests do not create delays.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-578$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team spends significant time answering requests for information that is already available in a dashboard, but users say the dashboard is 'too complicated.' What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Observe the common information users seek, identify where the dashboard creates friction, and test a simpler view, navigation or explanation targeted to those needs.$qb$, $qb$Train users to use the existing dashboard.$qb$, $qb$Create a document containing the most requested numbers.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team to stop answering questions already covered by the dashboard.$qb$, $qb$Add more dashboard features so users can find everything in one place.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-624$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team receives many requests marked 'urgent,' making it difficult to identify the few that genuinely require immediate action. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Analyze why requesters use the urgent label, define clear urgency criteria and consequences, and test a triage method that distinguishes true time-critical work.$qb$, $qb$Tell requesters to use 'urgent' less often.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to review every urgent request manually as soon as it arrives.$qb$, $qb$Create a second label called 'very urgent.'$qb$, $qb$Treat all requests as normal priority because the urgent label has lost meaning.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-670$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees repeatedly miss a small but important step even though it appears in the middle of a long procedure. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Observe when and why the step is missed, then test a point-of-action cue, workflow control, or procedure redesign that makes the critical step hard to overlook.$qb$, $qb$Highlight the step in bold in the procedure.$qb$, $qb$Send a reminder every month about the missed step.$qb$, $qb$Discipline employees who miss it more than once.$qb$, $qb$Add another person to check whether the step was completed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-716$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team repeatedly misses one field when transferring information from an email into a system. Training has already been repeated. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Observe the transfer step, identify why that field is easy to miss, and test a structured input, automation, or point-of-entry control that removes reliance on memory.$qb$, $qb$Run another training session focused on the missed field.$qb$, $qb$Add the field to a checklist beside every workstation.$qb$, $qb$Discipline repeat errors after the next reminder.$qb$, $qb$Assign one person to review every transferred record.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-762$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team keeps receiving requests with the wrong category selected, sending work to the wrong queue. Users say the category names are confusing. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Analyze which categories are confused, test clearer labels or guided questions, and measure whether routing errors fall before adding more manual correction.$qb$, $qb$Send users definitions of every category.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to reclassify requests after they arrive.$qb$, $qb$Add more categories so choices are more precise.$qb$, $qb$Tell users to read the guidance before selecting a category.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-808$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Customers frequently choose the wrong option in an online form even though the options are accurately described. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Observe how customers interpret the choices, identify the confusing distinction, and test clearer labels, examples or guided questions at the decision point.$qb$, $qb$Add more detailed descriptions below each option.$qb$, $qb$Tell support staff to correct the choice after submission.$qb$, $qb$Send customers a guide before they use the form.$qb$, $qb$Add a confirmation box saying they selected the correct option.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-001$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A conclusion that fits the available data may still be wrong if no one has looked for evidence that would ______ it.$qb$, $qb$contradict$qb$, $qb$repeat$qb$, $qb$complicate$qb$, $qb$confirm$qb$, $qb$support$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-002$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Two events that rise and fall together may share a hidden ______ rather than causing one another.$qb$, $qb$driver$qb$, $qb$threshold$qb$, $qb$average$qb$, $qb$label$qb$, $qb$interval$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-003$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$When a report presents only the cases that succeeded, the sample suffers from ______ that inflates the apparent result.$qb$, $qb$selection$qb$, $qb$rounding$qb$, $qb$transcription$qb$, $qb$duplication$qb$, $qb$sequencing$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-004$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Asking what evidence would change your mind converts an opinion into a ______ claim.$qb$, $qb$testable$qb$, $qb$popular$qb$, $qb$concise$qb$, $qb$categorical$qb$, $qb$documented$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-005$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$An argument can be entirely logical and still be unsound if one of its ______ is false.$qb$, $qb$premises$qb$, $qb$examples$qb$, $qb$footnotes$qb$, $qb$phrases$qb$, $qb$headings$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-006$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A single vivid example often carries more persuasive weight than statistics, which makes it a poor basis for ______ reasoning.$qb$, $qb$general$qb$, $qb$numerical$qb$, $qb$written$qb$, $qb$comparative$qb$, $qb$procedural$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-007$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Restating an opposing view in its strongest form before answering it tests whether you have understood the ______ rather than a caricature.$qb$, $qb$position$qb$, $qb$vocabulary$qb$, $qb$tone$qb$, $qb$chronology$qb$, $qb$audience$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-008$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A question phrased to suggest its own answer will produce a ______ response rather than an informative one.$qb$, $qb$biased$qb$, $qb$lengthy$qb$, $qb$narrow$qb$, $qb$quick$qb$, $qb$polite$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-009$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Treating an absence of evidence as evidence of absence is unsafe when nobody has actually ______ for it.$qb$, $qb$searched$qb$, $qb$waited$qb$, $qb$budgeted$qb$, $qb$filed$qb$, $qb$scheduled$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-010$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A forecast built on one scenario is fragile because it never tests what happens if the central ______ turns out wrong.$qb$, $qb$assumption$qb$, $qb$deadline$qb$, $qb$format$qb$, $qb$headline$qb$, $qb$summary$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-011$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Comparing a treated group with an untreated one is what allows an observed change to be attributed to the ______ rather than to time.$qb$, $qb$intervention$qb$, $qb$observer$qb$, $qb$difference$qb$, $qb$comparison$qb$, $qb$measurement$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-012$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Redefining a term mid-argument so that a challenged claim survives is a form of ______ reasoning.$qb$, $qb$evasive$qb$, $qb$numerical$qb$, $qb$sequential$qb$, $qb$procedural$qb$, $qb$comparative$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-013$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A metric that people are rewarded for improving will tend to be optimised directly, which erodes its value as a ______ of real performance.$qb$, $qb$proxy$qb$, $qb$record$qb$, $qb$target$qb$, $qb$cost$qb$, $qb$label$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-014$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Averages can conceal a group that is doing badly if the ______ within the data is never examined.$qb$, $qb$spread$qb$, $qb$heading$qb$, $qb$detail$qb$, $qb$average$qb$, $qb$total$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-015$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Reasoning that starts from the desired conclusion and gathers support for it afterwards is called ______ reasoning.$qb$, $qb$motivated$qb$, $qb$formal$qb$, $qb$correct$qb$, $qb$practical$qb$, $qb$confident$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-016$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A pattern found by testing many variables at once may be a product of ______ rather than a real relationship.$qb$, $qb$chance$qb$, $qb$formatting$qb$, $qb$bias$qb$, $qb$categorisation$qb$, $qb$ordering$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-FIB-017$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Before accepting a striking statistic, checking how the underlying quantity was ______ often explains most of the surprise.$qb$, $qb$defined$qb$, $qb$found$qb$, $qb$rounded$qb$, $qb$sorted$qb$, $qb$saved$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-018$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A vendor's case study shows every client improving after adopting their tool. What most limits the strength of this evidence?$qb$, $qb$Clients who saw no improvement may simply not appear among the published cases$qb$, $qb$The improvements reported may have been measured over differing time periods$qb$, $qb$The tool may work better in some industries than in the ones shown here$qb$, $qb$The case study was produced by a party with a commercial stake in the result$qb$, $qb$The number of clients described in the case study is fairly small overall$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-019$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Sales rose in the month after a new campaign launched. What is the most useful next check?$qb$, $qb$Whether sales rose by a similar amount in the same month of prior years$qb$, $qb$Whether the campaign reached the customers who accounted for the increase$qb$, $qb$Whether the sales increase held up in the months following the campaign$qb$, $qb$Whether the campaign budget was larger than that of previous campaigns$qb$, $qb$Whether the sales team reported the campaign as helpful to their work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-020$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two analysts reach opposite conclusions from the same dataset. What is the most productive first step?$qb$, $qb$Identify which assumptions each analyst applied before interpreting the figures$qb$, $qb$Ask each analyst to explain the reasoning that led to their stated conclusion$qb$, $qb$Check whether the two analysts were working from identical versions of the data$qb$, $qb$Bring in a third analyst to review both conclusions and settle the disagreement$qb$, $qb$Adopt the conclusion produced by whichever analyst has more relevant experience$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-021$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague argues that a policy must be effective because it is used by most competitors. What is the weakness here?$qb$, $qb$Widespread adoption can reflect imitation rather than any demonstrated benefit$qb$, $qb$Competitors may operate under conditions that differ from your own materially$qb$, $qb$The policy may have been adopted before evidence about it became available$qb$, $qb$Most competitors may have adopted it without ever measuring its actual effect$qb$, $qb$The number of competitors using the policy has not been established precisely$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-022$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An internal survey reports 85% satisfaction, but only 12% of staff responded. What should you conclude?$qb$, $qb$The result mainly describes those motivated enough to respond, not all staff$qb$, $qb$The result may still be sound if the respondents resemble the wider workforce$qb$, $qb$The result should be treated cautiously until the response rate is improved$qb$, $qb$The result is unreliable because the number of respondents was simply too low$qb$, $qb$The result is acceptable since surveys commonly attract low response levels$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-023$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A senior leader states a conclusion confidently but does not share the reasoning. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what evidence the conclusion rests on before building further work on it$qb$, $qb$Ask whether the reasoning can be shared so the team can apply it consistently$qb$, $qb$Accept the conclusion for now and look for supporting evidence independently$qb$, $qb$Accept the conclusion, since senior leaders usually have access to more context$qb$, $qb$Raise the concern with peers first to see whether others share the same doubt$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-024$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process change is followed by fewer defects, but staffing also increased that month. What does this mean?$qb$, $qb$The effect of the change cannot be separated from the effect of added staffing$qb$, $qb$The change is likely responsible, since it targeted defects and staffing did not$qb$, $qb$The defect reduction should be attributed partly to each of the two factors$qb$, $qb$The result needs another month of data before any conclusion can be reached$qb$, $qb$The staffing increase is the more plausible explanation of the two available$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-025$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Someone dismisses a report by pointing out that its author has a conflict of interest. How should you treat this?$qb$, $qb$Treat it as a reason to scrutinise the evidence, not as a refutation of it$qb$, $qb$Treat it as relevant context that raises the standard of proof the report needs$qb$, $qb$Treat it as grounds to seek a second report from an independent source$qb$, $qb$Treat it as sufficient reason to set the report aside from consideration$qb$, $qb$Treat it as irrelevant, since the evidence stands apart from its author$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-026$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A dashboard shows a metric improving while frontline staff report conditions worsening. What is the best interpretation?$qb$, $qb$The metric may be capturing something narrower than the outcome that matters$qb$, $qb$The metric and the staff reports may be describing different time periods$qb$, $qb$The staff reports may reflect a vocal minority rather than general experience$qb$, $qb$The metric is more reliable because it is quantitative rather than anecdotal$qb$, $qb$The discrepancy will likely resolve itself as more data becomes available$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-027$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are asked to evaluate a proposal that assumes demand will grow 20% annually. What matters most?$qb$, $qb$What the growth assumption rests on and how the proposal fares if it is wrong$qb$, $qb$Whether historical demand growth in this market has been near that level$qb$, $qb$Whether the proposal includes any alternative scenario for lower growth$qb$, $qb$Whether the person who wrote the proposal has forecast accurately before$qb$, $qb$Whether 20% is consistent with the growth rates competitors are assuming$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-028$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague supports a claim by citing an article, but the article cites no primary source. What follows?$qb$, $qb$The citation moves the claim one step back without adding evidential weight$qb$, $qb$The citation is weaker than a primary source but is not entirely without value$qb$, $qb$The citation should prompt a search for whatever original work underlies it$qb$, $qb$The citation is acceptable if the publication carrying it is well regarded$qb$, $qb$The citation is adequate, since most working references operate this way$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-029$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Results from a pilot in one department are used to justify a company-wide rollout. What is the main risk?$qb$, $qb$Conditions that made the pilot succeed may not exist in other departments$qb$, $qb$The pilot may have run for too short a period to show durable effects$qb$, $qb$The pilot department may have been chosen because success was likely$qb$, $qb$The rollout may cost considerably more than the pilot did to operate$qb$, $qb$The pilot results may not have been measured consistently throughout$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-030$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You notice you feel more convinced by a report because it confirms your existing view. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Apply the same scrutiny you would give a report reaching the opposite result$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague who holds a different view to examine the report as well$qb$, $qb$Set the report aside briefly and return to it with the question reframed$qb$, $qb$Note the reaction and proceed, since the report may well be correct anyway$qb$, $qb$Discount the report somewhat to compensate for the agreement you noticed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-031$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A claim is supported by an analogy to a different industry. How much weight should the analogy carry?$qb$, $qb$It carries weight only where the features that drive the outcome also transfer$qb$, $qb$It carries weight as illustration but not as evidence for the claim itself$qb$, $qb$It carries some weight, though less than direct evidence from this industry$qb$, $qb$It carries weight if the other industry is broadly similar in size and structure$qb$, $qb$It carries little weight, since industries differ too much for useful comparison$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-032$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two variables move together closely for three years and then diverge. What does the divergence suggest?$qb$, $qb$The earlier relationship may have depended on a condition that has now changed$qb$, $qb$The earlier relationship may have been coincidental rather than causal at all$qb$, $qb$One of the two variables may now be affected by a newly introduced factor$qb$, $qb$The measurement method for one variable may have changed at that point$qb$, $qb$The relationship will likely reassert itself once conditions normalise again$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-033$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A number is quoted as 'up to 40% faster'. What does this phrasing tell you?$qb$, $qb$It reports the best observed case, which may be far from the typical one$qb$, $qb$It reports a ceiling rather than an average, so the range remains unknown$qb$, $qb$It reports a figure that was achieved under some particular set of conditions$qb$, $qb$It reports a genuine improvement, though the exact amount is left unclear$qb$, $qb$It reports a marketing figure and should therefore be disregarded entirely$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-MCQ-034$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must decide with incomplete information and no time to gather more. What is the soundest approach?$qb$, $qb$Name the assumptions you are relying on and what would signal they are wrong$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that performs acceptably across the plausible scenarios$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that experienced colleagues consider most likely to work$qb$, $qb$Choose the option with the strongest evidence among what is currently held$qb$, $qb$Delay as long as the deadline allows in case further information arrives$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-035$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team has built a business case on a market-size figure taken from a single consultancy report. The figure is now central to a funding request due next week. How do you proceed?$qb$, $qb$Trace the figure to its original basis, check it against an independent source, and state the resulting range in the case$qb$, $qb$Check the figure against one other available source and record in the case that this estimate carries real uncertainty$qb$, $qb$Flag to the team that the figure rests on one source and propose adding a caveat before the request is submitted$qb$, $qb$Proceed with the figure since the consultancy is reputable, and mention the single-source basis only if a reviewer asks$qb$, $qb$Proceed with the figure unchanged, since the deadline leaves no realistic time to verify market-size estimates properly$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-036$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A respected colleague presents an analysis you believe contains a reasoning error, in a meeting with senior leaders present. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Ask a specific question about the step you doubt, so the reasoning gets examined openly rather than as a personal challenge$qb$, $qb$Note the concern now and raise it with the colleague directly after the meeting, before anyone acts on the analysis$qb$, $qb$Ask the group whether anyone else has reviewed the analysis, letting the question surface without naming the issue itself$qb$, $qb$Say nothing in the meeting and send the colleague your concern in writing once you have checked your own reasoning$qb$, $qb$Defer to the colleague's judgement, since they know the subject better and the leaders present seem satisfied with it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-037$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are given a dataset and a conclusion, and asked to prepare slides supporting that conclusion for a board meeting. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Prepare the slides, and show the requester where the data qualifies the conclusion so the board sees those limits too$qb$, $qb$Prepare the slides as asked, including a clearly marked section on the limits the data places on this conclusion$qb$, $qb$Ask the requester whether the conclusion came from this data or was arrived at before the data was examined$qb$, $qb$Prepare the slides as asked, since deciding what a board should conclude is the requester's responsibility and not yours$qb$, $qb$Prepare the slides from only the parts of the data supporting the conclusion, since that is what the request calls for$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-038$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two departments give you conflicting figures for the same operational metric, and both insist theirs is correct. How do you resolve it?$qb$, $qb$Compare how each department defines and collects the metric, since definitional differences usually explain conflicts of this kind$qb$, $qb$Ask both departments to walk through their calculation method so the point of divergence can be located precisely$qb$, $qb$Take the figure from the department owning the process, since they sit closest to the activity being measured here$qb$, $qb$Average the two figures and note the discrepancy, so the report can proceed without stalling on this disagreement$qb$, $qb$Escalate to both department heads and use whichever figure the two of them jointly agree to endorse in writing$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-039$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A widely accepted assumption underpins how your team plans capacity, but you have found data suggesting it no longer holds. How do you proceed?$qb$, $qb$Set out what the data shows, what follows if the assumption has changed, and propose a specific test before revising$qb$, $qb$Raise the finding with your manager and suggest the assumption be re-examined before the next planning cycle begins$qb$, $qb$Share the data with the team and ask whether others have observed anything consistent with the same conclusion$qb$, $qb$Keep planning on the existing assumption while gathering more data, since one dataset is unlikely to overturn it$qb$, $qb$Adjust your own planning to reflect the new data and let the wider team continue on the established assumption$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-040$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are reviewing a proposal whose author has clearly worked hard on it, but whose central argument rests on a flawed comparison. How do you give feedback?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the work, show precisely where the comparison breaks down, and name the evidence that would repair it$qb$, $qb$Point out the problem with the comparison directly and ask the author how they would address it in a revision$qb$, $qb$Frame the issue as a question about the comparison, so the author reaches the problem through their own reasoning$qb$, $qb$Praise the overall effort and mention the comparison issue briefly, so the author is not discouraged by the review$qb$, $qb$Approve the proposal with a note about the comparison, since the author's effort and intent are evidently sound$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-041$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI tool your team uses produces a well-written analysis that reaches a conclusion nobody expected. How do you treat it?$qb$, $qb$Check its reasoning and sources, since fluent writing tells you nothing about whether the analysis is actually correct$qb$, $qb$Verify the key factual claims independently before this analysis informs any decision the team is about to make$qb$, $qb$Ask the tool to explain its reasoning step by step, then assess whether that explanation holds together properly$qb$, $qb$Treat the unexpected conclusion as a useful prompt and investigate the underlying question yourself from the start$qb$, $qb$Accept the analysis provisionally, since this tool has produced reliable and accurate output on previous occasions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-042$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your organisation is about to repeat a decision that failed two years ago, and few current staff remember the earlier attempt. How do you intervene?$qb$, $qb$Set out what was tried, what happened, and which conditions have changed, so the decision carries that record$qb$, $qb$Raise the earlier attempt with the decision-makers and ask what is different this time about the proposed approach$qb$, $qb$Locate documentation of the earlier attempt and circulate it to the team before this decision is finally settled$qb$, $qb$Mention that something similar was tried before and suggest that someone look into what eventually came of it$qb$, $qb$Let the decision proceed, since conditions have likely changed enough to make the earlier outcome largely irrelevant$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-043$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder asks you to remove a caveat from your report because it 'weakens the message' before it goes to a client. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Explain what the caveat protects against, and offer to reword it more clearly without dropping the qualification$qb$, $qb$Decline to remove it and explain the risk that omission would create if the client relied on this report$qb$, $qb$Ask what specifically concerns them about the wording and work toward a version that both of you can accept$qb$, $qb$Move the caveat to an appendix so the main message reads cleanly while the qualification remains available there$qb$, $qb$Remove the caveat as requested, since the stakeholder owns the client relationship and understands their needs$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-044$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A long-running weekly report takes your team a full day to produce, and you cannot find anyone who uses it. How do you proceed?$qb$, $qb$Ask the recipients directly what decisions the report informs, then propose stopping it if no decision depends on it$qb$, $qb$Pause the report for a set period and see whether anyone raises its absence before deciding whether to resume it$qb$, $qb$Raise it with your manager and ask whether the report can be dropped or reduced in scope going forward$qb$, $qb$Reduce the report to a shorter summary, since some recipients may be reading it without ever saying so$qb$, $qb$Continue producing it, since a long-standing report likely serves a purpose that is not visible to your team$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-045$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier's quality figures look strong, but you notice the sample they report on is chosen by the supplier themselves. How do you act?$qb$, $qb$Ask how the sample is drawn, and request figures from a batch that you or a third party select instead$qb$, $qb$Request the underlying data for all batches so the reported figures can be checked against the full record$qb$, $qb$Raise the sampling method with the supplier and ask them to explain how representativeness is ensured$qb$, $qb$Accept the figures for now but arrange your own spot checks on incoming batches over the coming quarter$qb$, $qb$Accept the figures, since a supplier reporting poor sampling would be damaging their own commercial interest$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-046$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team reaches consensus quickly on a significant decision, and nobody has raised an objection. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Ask someone to argue the strongest case against it, so agreement is tested rather than assumed to be sound$qb$, $qb$Ask directly what would have to be true for this decision to turn out badly, and see what surfaces$qb$, $qb$Invite anyone with reservations to raise them privately, in case the group setting is discouraging dissent$qb$, $qb$Record the decision along with a note that no objections were raised during the discussion of it$qb$, $qb$Proceed with the decision, since rapid consensus suggests the right answer was clear to everyone involved$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-047$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client insists that a problem has one cause, but your analysis points to several interacting factors. How do you present this?$qb$, $qb$Show what each factor contributes and what fixing only their preferred cause would leave unresolved$qb$, $qb$Present the full analysis and explain why single-cause explanations rarely fit problems of this kind$qb$, $qb$Present their cause first as one real factor, then introduce the others as additional contributors$qb$, $qb$Present the analysis but lead with their preferred cause, since that is what they expect to see$qb$, $qb$Present the single cause they identified, since the client understands their own operation best here$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-048$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are asked to summarise a long research report for leadership, and the findings are more mixed than the executive summary suggests. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Summarise what the evidence supports and where it is contested, noting that the report's own summary overstates it$qb$, $qb$Summarise the findings as the body of the report presents them, rather than following its executive summary$qb$, $qb$Summarise the report's conclusions and add a note that the underlying evidence is more mixed than stated$qb$, $qb$Summarise the executive summary, since that is what the report's own authors chose to highlight for readers$qb$, $qb$Summarise the strongest findings only, since leadership needs a clear message rather than a qualified one$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-049$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Halfway through a project, evidence emerges that the original approach is unlikely to work, but significant effort is already invested. How do you proceed?$qb$, $qb$Assess the remaining cost and likely outcome from here, setting aside what has already been spent on it$qb$, $qb$Present the new evidence to the sponsors along with the options and their expected outcomes from this point$qb$, $qb$Propose a limited test of the alternative approach before committing to abandoning the original one$qb$, $qb$Continue while looking for ways to salvage the work already done, since the investment was substantial$qb$, $qb$Continue as planned, since changing course midway would waste the effort the team has already put in$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-CT-SJT-050$qb$, $qb$A2-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realise a recommendation you made three months ago was based on an error, and it has since been implemented. How do you act?$qb$, $qb$Raise it promptly with what the error was, what follows from it now, and what you propose doing about it$qb$, $qb$Raise the error with your manager immediately and ask how they would prefer the correction to be handled$qb$, $qb$Assess how much the error actually changed the outcome, then report it with that assessment attached to it$qb$, $qb$Monitor the situation to see whether the error causes real harm before raising it with anyone else involved$qb$, $qb$Correct your approach going forward without reopening the earlier decision, which has already been made and acted on$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-FIB-001$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A choice that is easy to undo later needs less study than one that is hard to ______.$qb$, $qb$reverse$qb$, $qb$explain$qb$, $qb$announce$qb$, $qb$record$qb$, $qb$price$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-FIB-002$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Waiting for complete information has a cost, because the delay itself carries ______.$qb$, $qb$risk$qb$, $qb$interest$qb$, $qb$doubt$qb$, $qb$safety$qb$, $qb$comfort$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-FIB-003$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$When the future is unclear, a plan that holds up in several situations beats one built for a single ______.$qb$, $qb$forecast$qb$, $qb$decision$qb$, $qb$failure$qb$, $qb$word$qb$, $qb$department$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-FIB-004$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Trying an idea on a small scale first lowers the cost of turning out to be ______.$qb$, $qb$wrong$qb$, $qb$busy$qb$, $qb$early$qb$, $qb$slow$qb$, $qb$cautious$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-FIB-005$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Writing down what you expect to happen lets you check later whether your ______ was sound.$qb$, $qb$reasoning$qb$, $qb$timing$qb$, $qb$wording$qb$, $qb$record$qb$, $qb$format$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-FIB-006$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Two options may look equally good on average, yet differ sharply in how bad their worst ______ is.$qb$, $qb$case$qb$, $qb$quarter$qb$, $qb$month$qb$, $qb$review$qb$, $qb$time$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
