  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-FIB-011$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A decision made under time pressure should record what was known then, so it can be judged fairly rather than by its ______.$qb$, $qb$outcome$qb$, $qb$length$qb$, $qb$cost$qb$, $qb$author$qb$, $qb$speed$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-FIB-012$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Spreading a large commitment into smaller steps lets you stop early if the early steps go ______.$qb$, $qb$badly$qb$, $qb$public$qb$, $qb$quiet$qb$, $qb$formal$qb$, $qb$routine$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-FIB-013$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$When nobody can know the answer, the honest position is to state the range rather than a single ______.$qb$, $qb$number$qb$, $qb$promise$qb$, $qb$headline$qb$, $qb$deadline$qb$, $qb$source$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-FIB-014$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A choice that looks safe because nothing changes can still carry the risk of ______.$qb$, $qb$inaction$qb$, $qb$expense$qb$, $qb$comfort$qb$, $qb$routine$qb$, $qb$visibility$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-FIB-015$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Comparing what you would lose if you act and are wrong against what you would lose if you wait is a way of weighing ______.$qb$, $qb$tradeoffs$qb$, $qb$patience$qb$, $qb$opinions$qb$, $qb$roles$qb$, $qb$timelines$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-FIB-016$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$If new information would not change what you do, gathering more of it mostly costs ______.$qb$, $qb$time$qb$, $qb$paper$qb$, $qb$storage$qb$, $qb$attention$qb$, $qb$filing$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-018$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must choose a supplier this week, and neither option can be fully checked in time. What matters most?$qb$, $qb$Which choice would be easier to move away from if it turns out to be wrong$qb$, $qb$Which choice has the stronger evidence among the limited information you hold$qb$, $qb$Which choice carries the smaller loss if the supplier fails to deliver on time$qb$, $qb$Which choice comes from the supplier with the longer history in this market$qb$, $qb$Which choice the team feels more comfortable with after discussing both options$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-019$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A project has three possible outcomes and you cannot tell which is likely. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Work out what you would do under each outcome, and see which action fits more than one$qb$, $qb$Work out which outcome is most likely so the plan can be built around that one$qb$, $qb$Work out which outcome would hurt most, and plan mainly to protect against that$qb$, $qb$Work out how long it would take to gather the information needed to tell them apart$qb$, $qb$Work out which outcome the team expects, since they know the work best of all$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-020$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are asked for a single delivery date, but the work has real unknowns in it. How should you answer?$qb$, $qb$Give a range with the main unknowns named, so the date can be firmed up as they resolve$qb$, $qb$Give an honest earliest and latest date, and explain what sits between the two ends$qb$, $qb$Give a date you are confident about, with a note that it may move if problems appear$qb$, $qb$Give the date the requester needs, and flag any slippage as soon as it becomes visible$qb$, $qb$Give an optimistic date, since setting an ambitious target usually pulls delivery forward$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-021$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A decision looks good on expected value, but the worst case would end the business. What follows?$qb$, $qb$Expected value is the wrong test here, because the business cannot survive to average out$qb$, $qb$The worst case should be reduced first, then the expected value test can be applied$qb$, $qb$The decision needs a much higher bar than expected value alone would set for it$qb$, $qb$The decision should be split into stages so the worst case never arrives all at once$qb$, $qb$The decision is acceptable if the worst case is unlikely enough to be worth the risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-022$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two team members give confident but opposite predictions about a market. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask each what they would expect to see if they were wrong, then watch for those signs$qb$, $qb$Ask each to explain what their prediction rests on, and compare the two sets of reasons$qb$, $qb$Ask whether there is a plan that would work reasonably well under either prediction$qb$, $qb$Ask a third person with market experience to say which prediction seems more likely$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to vote, so the decision reflects the group's overall view of the market$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-023$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You made a good decision using the information you had, and it turned out badly. How should it be reviewed?$qb$, $qb$Judge the reasoning against what was known then, and note separately what the result taught$qb$, $qb$Judge the reasoning first, then ask whether any available information was missed at the time$qb$, $qb$Judge both the reasoning and the outcome, since results matter as well as the process$qb$, $qb$Judge mainly the outcome, since the purpose of a decision is to produce a good result$qb$, $qb$Judge it as a poor decision, because a sound process would have produced a better result$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-024$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A pilot in one site succeeded, and you must decide about a wider rollout. What is the key question?$qb$, $qb$Whether the things that made the pilot work are also present at the other sites$qb$, $qb$Whether the pilot ran long enough for its results to be treated as settled$qb$, $qb$Whether the pilot site was chosen because success there was more likely$qb$, $qb$Whether the rollout can be staged so that early sites can be reviewed first$qb$, $qb$Whether the cost of the rollout is proportionate to the gain the pilot showed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-025$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team wants more data before committing, but the window to act closes in two days. What is soundest?$qb$, $qb$Ask what specific data would change the choice, and whether it can arrive in two days$qb$, $qb$Ask what the team would decide if no further data arrived, and test that choice$qb$, $qb$Ask whether a smaller version of the commitment could be made within the window$qb$, $qb$Ask for an extension to the window so the data can be gathered properly first$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to decide now, since more data rarely changes an already clear picture$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-026$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A forecast has been accurate for five years and has just missed badly. What should you conclude?$qb$, $qb$Something in the conditions the forecast relies on may have changed and needs checking$qb$, $qb$One miss does not overturn five years, but it is a reason to review the method$qb$, $qb$The forecast may still be sound, since even good methods miss occasionally by chance$qb$, $qb$The forecast should be replaced, since a bad miss shows the method has stopped working$qb$, $qb$The forecast should be kept, since its long record outweighs a single poor year$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-027$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are choosing between a safe small gain and a risky large one, for a decision you can repeat many times. What matters?$qb$, $qb$That a repeatable decision lets the odds work out, so the risky option may be right$qb$, $qb$That the risky option should be tested at small scale before it is used widely$qb$, $qb$That the two options should be compared on their average result across many tries$qb$, $qb$That the safe option protects the team from a run of bad results early on$qb$, $qb$That the safer option is preferable, since steady gains are easier to plan around$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-028$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A senior leader asks you to remove the uncertainty range from a slide. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explain what the range protects against, and offer a simpler way to show the same thing$qb$, $qb$Keep the range and explain the risk if others treat a single number as settled fact$qb$, $qb$Ask what specifically concerns them about it, and look for a version both can accept$qb$, $qb$Move the range into the notes so the slide stays clean and the detail is still there$qb$, $qb$Remove it as asked, since the leader knows what the audience needs to see$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-029$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Halfway through a plan, you learn something that would have changed the original choice. What now?$qb$, $qb$Compare the options from here forward, ignoring what has already been spent$qb$, $qb$Bring the new information to the sponsors with the options open from this point$qb$, $qb$Test the alternative in a small way before deciding whether to switch fully$qb$, $qb$Look for a way to adapt the current plan so the earlier work still counts$qb$, $qb$Continue as planned, since changing course now would waste the effort already made$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-030$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You must set a budget for something that has never been done in your organisation. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Find the closest comparable work elsewhere, and add room for what is different here$qb$, $qb$Build the estimate from the parts you can size, and mark the parts you cannot$qb$, $qb$Set a range rather than a figure, and agree a point where it will be revisited$qb$, $qb$Set the figure the sponsor expects, and raise it if the work proves larger$qb$, $qb$Set a generous figure, since underestimating causes more trouble than overestimating$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-031$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A risk is unlikely but would be very costly. Your team wants to ignore it. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Ask what it would cost to reduce the damage if it happened, not just to prevent it$qb$, $qb$Ask how the team would recover if it did happen, and whether that answer is acceptable$qb$, $qb$Ask for the risk to be recorded with a review date, rather than dropped entirely$qb$, $qb$Agree to set it aside for now, since effort is better spent on likelier risks$qb$, $qb$Agree to ignore it, since planning for unlikely events uses time the team does not have$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-032$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two options are close, and further analysis is not resolving the gap. What is the best move?$qb$, $qb$Choose the one that leaves more room to change course, and set a date to review$qb$, $qb$Choose either and commit, since the analysis suggests the difference is small$qb$, $qb$Choose the one the people doing the work would rather carry out in practice$qb$, $qb$Continue the analysis, since a clear winner may emerge with a little more work$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that was proposed first, since it has had more thought behind it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-033$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are told an outcome is 70% likely. What does this mean for planning?$qb$, $qb$That the other outcome happens roughly three times in ten, so it needs a plan too$qb$, $qb$That the likelier outcome should shape the plan, with a fallback held ready$qb$, $qb$That the estimate itself may be uncertain and worth testing before relying on it$qb$, $qb$That the plan should be built for the likely outcome, since it will usually happen$qb$, $qb$That the plan can treat the outcome as settled, since 70% is a strong majority$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-MCQ-034$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A decision must be made now, and you will be judged on it later. What should you record?$qb$, $qb$What you knew, what you assumed, and what would have made you choose differently$qb$, $qb$What information was available at the time and what the main options were$qb$, $qb$What the reasons for the choice were, so they can be explained afterwards$qb$, $qb$What the expected outcome is, so progress can be measured against it later$qb$, $qb$What the deadline was, so the pressure you were under is understood afterwards$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-035$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier offers a large discount if you sign a three-year contract by Friday. You cannot properly check their financial health in time. How do you proceed?$qb$, $qb$Ask for a shorter term or an exit clause, so the saving is kept without locking in the risk$qb$, $qb$Do what checking is possible in the time, and decide with the gaps clearly written down$qb$, $qb$Ask the supplier to extend the deadline so the checks can be completed properly first$qb$, $qb$Sign for the discount, since the saving is certain and the risk is only a possibility$qb$, $qb$Let the offer lapse, since committing for three years without checks is not defensible$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-036$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team must pick a technology platform now, but the market may look very different in two years. How do you approach it?$qb$, $qb$Weigh how hard each option would be to move away from, alongside how well it works today$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that meets current needs and set a date to review the choice again$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that most competitors are using, since that reduces the chance of being stranded$qb$, $qb$Choose the strongest option on today's requirements, since future needs cannot be known$qb$, $qb$Delay the choice until the market direction becomes clearer over the next few months$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-037$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer asks whether a fix will work, and you genuinely do not know. How do you answer?$qb$, $qb$Say what you do know, what you are testing, and when you will be able to answer$qb$, $qb$Say that you are not certain yet, and give the date by which you expect to know$qb$, $qb$Say that you believe it will work, and explain what you are doing to confirm that$qb$, $qb$Say it should work, since expressing doubt would worry the customer unnecessarily$qb$, $qb$Say that it will work, and manage the situation if it later turns out otherwise$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-038$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks for your recommendation on a decision where the evidence is genuinely mixed. How do you present it?$qb$, $qb$Give a clear recommendation, and set out what would have to be true for it to be wrong$qb$, $qb$Give the recommendation along with the main evidence on each side of the question$qb$, $qb$Give both options with their strengths, and let your manager weigh them and decide$qb$, $qb$Give the recommendation only, since your manager wants a clear answer rather than detail$qb$, $qb$Give the option that carries less risk, since mixed evidence argues for the safer path$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-039$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project's success depends on an approval you cannot control and cannot predict. How do you plan?$qb$, $qb$Plan the work so the parts not needing approval move ahead, and the rest waits ready$qb$, $qb$Plan for both outcomes and agree in advance what happens under each of them$qb$, $qb$Plan on the assumption approval comes, while tracking how likely that now looks$qb$, $qb$Plan on the assumption it comes, since planning for refusal slows the team down$qb$, $qb$Hold the project until the approval decision is known, so effort is not wasted$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-040$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Early results from a new initiative are weak, but it was always expected to take a year. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Check the results against what was expected at this point, not against the final target$qb$, $qb$Ask what early signs would show it is on track, and see whether those are present$qb$, $qb$Give it more time as planned, while watching whether the gap is widening or closing$qb$, $qb$Report the weak results and let the sponsors decide whether to continue with it$qb$, $qb$Consider stopping it, since weak early results usually point to a weak overall outcome$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-041$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague pushes for a decision to be delayed until more is known. You believe delay is itself risky. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Set out what the delay costs and what it buys, so both risks are compared openly$qb$, $qb$Ask what specifically would be known later that would change the choice being made$qb$, $qb$Propose a short fixed delay with a decision date, so waiting does not become open-ended$qb$, $qb$Agree to the delay, since your colleague may be seeing a risk you have not considered$qb$, $qb$Press for the decision now, since delaying decisions tends to make them harder later$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-042$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are asked to commit resources to a plan that depends on an assumption nobody can test. How do you proceed?$qb$, $qb$State the assumption plainly, and agree what would count as a sign it is not holding$qb$, $qb$Commit in stages, so the exposure grows only as the assumption proves out over time$qb$, $qb$Ask whether the plan can be reshaped so it depends on that assumption less heavily$qb$, $qb$Commit as asked, and raise the assumption if it later appears to be causing problems$qb$, $qb$Commit fully, since untestable assumptions are common and plans cannot wait for certainty$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-043$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two departments want opposite decisions, and both have reasonable cases. The deadline is tomorrow. How do you resolve it?$qb$, $qb$Find what both cases agree on, and test whether a choice exists that both could accept$qb$, $qb$Ask each to name what they most need protected, then decide on that basis$qb$, $qb$Take the decision to the person who can settle it, with both cases set out fairly$qb$, $qb$Choose the department whose case is stronger, and explain the reasoning to the other$qb$, $qb$Split the difference so both departments get part of what they were asking for$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-044$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realise the plan you are running has no point at which anyone would say it is failing. How do you fix it?$qb$, $qb$Agree now what result by what date would mean stopping, and write it into the plan$qb$, $qb$Propose review points where the plan is checked against what was expected by then$qb$, $qb$Raise it with the sponsor and ask what evidence would change their view of the plan$qb$, $qb$Add regular progress reporting so problems become visible as the work continues$qb$, $qb$Continue as planned, since the team will notice soon enough if the work is failing$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-045$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A funder asks for a confident forecast, but your honest view is that the range is wide. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Give the range and explain what drives it, so the funder can judge the risk themselves$qb$, $qb$Give your best single figure with the range beside it, and say which parts are uncertain$qb$, $qb$Give the figure they need, and explain the main things that could move it either way$qb$, $qb$Give a confident figure, since funders need certainty and the range would unsettle them$qb$, $qb$Give the most favourable figure in the range, since that is the one they are hoping for$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-046$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team has been asked to guarantee an outcome that depends partly on a third party. How do you handle the request?$qb$, $qb$Commit to what you control, and set out clearly what depends on the third party$qb$, $qb$Explain which parts you can guarantee and which carry risk from outside the team$qb$, $qb$Ask whether the third party can give their own commitment before you give yours$qb$, $qb$Give the guarantee and manage the third party closely to make sure they deliver$qb$, $qb$Give the guarantee, since raising doubts now would suggest the team lacks confidence$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-047$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An option is available today at a good price but will not be available again. You are not ready to decide. How do you act?$qb$, $qb$Ask what it would cost to hold the option open, and weigh that against deciding blind$qb$, $qb$Work out the least you would need to know to decide, and see if you can get it today$qb$, $qb$Decide on what you have, accepting that some information will remain missing$qb$, $qb$Take the option, since a good price that will not return is worth some uncertainty$qb$, $qb$Let it pass, since deciding without being ready is how poor commitments get made$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-048$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are running a decision meeting and one senior voice is closing down discussion of the risks. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Ask the group directly what would have to go wrong for this to fail, and record the answers$qb$, $qb$Ask each person in turn for one concern, so the quieter views also reach the table$qb$, $qb$Note the risks yourself and circulate them in writing after the meeting has ended$qb$, $qb$Speak to the senior person afterwards about leaving more room for the risk discussion$qb$, $qb$Let the discussion close, since the senior person likely has context others do not$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-049$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A decision you took six months ago is now clearly wrong, though it was reasonable then. Someone raises it publicly. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Say what was known then, what has changed, and what you would do differently now$qb$, $qb$Explain the reasoning at the time and accept that the outcome has proved poor$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the outcome and move the discussion to what should happen from here$qb$, $qb$Explain that the decision was reasonable given what was known, and leave it there$qb$, $qb$Point out that nobody could have predicted the change, so the criticism is unfair$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-DMU-SJT-050$qb$, $qb$A2-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your organisation wants a single number for a risk that cannot honestly be measured. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Offer a scale or a range with the reasoning shown, rather than a number implying precision$qb$, $qb$Give a number with its basis stated clearly, so nobody treats it as measured fact$qb$, $qb$Explain why the risk cannot be measured, and propose what could be tracked instead$qb$, $qb$Give the number requested, with a note that it is an estimate rather than a measurement$qb$, $qb$Give a number, since a rough figure is more useful to the organisation than none$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-001$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A problem that keeps coming back is usually a sign that last time someone fixed only the ______.$qb$, $qb$symptom$qb$, $qb$paperwork$qb$, $qb$delay$qb$, $qb$report$qb$, $qb$blame$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
