  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-003$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$When people quietly build their own way around a system, that shortcut points to a hidden ______.$qb$, $qb$gap$qb$, $qb$habit$qb$, $qb$delay$qb$, $qb$cost$qb$, $qb$rule$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-004$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Watching how a job is really done often shows differences from the way the process is ______.$qb$, $qb$written$qb$, $qb$timed$qb$, $qb$priced$qb$, $qb$approved$qb$, $qb$assigned$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-005$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Counting how often a small problem happens turns a complaint into ______.$qb$, $qb$evidence$qb$, $qb$pressure$qb$, $qb$opinion$qb$, $qb$history$qb$, $qb$routine$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-006$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$The first idea that comes to mind is often the most familiar one rather than the most ______.$qb$, $qb$useful$qb$, $qb$recent$qb$, $qb$popular$qb$, $qb$detailed$qb$, $qb$expensive$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-007$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Describing a problem without naming a fix keeps the search for ideas ______.$qb$, $qb$open$qb$, $qb$brief$qb$, $qb$fair$qb$, $qb$slow$qb$, $qb$quiet$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-008$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A step that everyone finds annoying but nobody mentions has probably stopped being ______.$qb$, $qb$noticed$qb$, $qb$timed$qb$, $qb$priced$qb$, $qb$documented$qb$, $qb$scheduled$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-009$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Asking the people who do the work every day often uncovers problems that never reach a ______.$qb$, $qb$manager$qb$, $qb$pattern$qb$, $qb$system$qb$, $qb$customer$qb$, $qb$supplier$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-010$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Trying a small change on one shift first keeps the cost of a failed idea ______.$qb$, $qb$low$qb$, $qb$public$qb$, $qb$brief$qb$, $qb$hidden$qb$, $qb$shared$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-011$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A new idea is easier to judge when someone explains the problem it is meant to ______.$qb$, $qb$solve$qb$, $qb$price$qb$, $qb$record$qb$, $qb$describe$qb$, $qb$replace$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-012$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Two teams solving the same problem separately is itself a sign of poor ______.$qb$, $qb$coordination$qb$, $qb$pricing$qb$, $qb$reporting$qb$, $qb$training$qb$, $qb$staffing$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-013$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$When a rule causes more trouble than the problem it prevents, it may be worth ______ it.$qb$, $qb$revisiting$qb$, $qb$printing$qb$, $qb$explaining$qb$, $qb$enforcing$qb$, $qb$repeating$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-014$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Copying a fix from another team works better when you first check what makes the two situations ______.$qb$, $qb$different$qb$, $qb$famous$qb$, $qb$connected$qb$, $qb$successful$qb$, $qb$important$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-015$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Writing down what a change is meant to achieve makes it possible to tell later whether it ______.$qb$, $qb$worked$qb$, $qb$ended$qb$, $qb$finished$qb$, $qb$spread$qb$, $qb$cost$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-016$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Complaints about many different things can sometimes share one common ______.$qb$, $qb$cause$qb$, $qb$supplier$qb$, $qb$team$qb$, $qb$season$qb$, $qb$customer$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-FIB-017$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$An idea that would take a year to test is harder to learn from than one that gives an answer ______.$qb$, $qb$quickly$qb$, $qb$loudly$qb$, $qb$publicly$qb$, $qb$formally$qb$, $qb$silently$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-018$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A café keeps running out of milk on Saturday mornings. The manager orders more milk each week, but it still runs out. What is the most useful thing to look at next?$qb$, $qb$Why Saturday demand is different, since ordering more has not solved the problem$qb$, $qb$How much milk is actually used each day of the week, not just the weekly total$qb$, $qb$Whether the delivery arrives late on Fridays and leaves less stock for Saturday$qb$, $qb$Whether staff are wasting milk during the busy Saturday morning period$qb$, $qb$Whether the supplier can deliver a second time on Saturday to cover the gap$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-019$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$In a warehouse, pickers often walk to the far shelves for the most common items. Nobody has changed the layout in years. What does this suggest?$qb$, $qb$The layout may have been set for a stock mix that has since changed$qb$, $qb$The layout may never have been designed around how often items are picked$qb$, $qb$The walking time is a real cost that has simply not been measured$qb$, $qb$The pickers may not have been trained in the fastest route through the aisles$qb$, $qb$The layout is probably fine, since experienced pickers have adapted to it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-020$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A hospital ward finds that discharge paperwork is often late. Three different staff each blame a different step. What should you do first?$qb$, $qb$Follow one discharge from start to finish and see where the time actually goes$qb$, $qb$Ask all three to describe their step, and look for where the handovers break$qb$, $qb$Time each of the three steps separately to see which one takes longest$qb$, $qb$Ask the ward manager which of the three explanations they think is right$qb$, $qb$Add a reminder system so staff complete their step on time in future$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-021$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A delivery driver has worked out a faster route than the one the system gives. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Find out why the system's route is slower, since other drivers may face the same$qb$, $qb$Check whether the driver's route is safe and legal, then share it with the team$qb$, $qb$Ask the driver to explain the route so it can be compared with the system's$qb$, $qb$Let the driver use their own route, since they know the area better$qb$, $qb$Ask the driver to follow the system route, since it was set for good reasons$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-022$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A school office gets many parent calls asking the same question about term dates. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Look at why parents cannot find the answer themselves before adding more staff$qb$, $qb$Check where term dates are published and whether parents are being told about it$qb$, $qb$Count how many of the calls are about this one question over a month$qb$, $qb$Prepare a standard answer so staff can deal with the calls more quickly$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to be patient, since parent calls are a normal part of the job$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-023$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A workshop has a checklist that everyone signs but few actually read. What does this tell you?$qb$, $qb$The checklist has become a formality, so it is no longer catching anything$qb$, $qb$The checklist may be too long or unclear for people to use while working$qb$, $qb$The signing step is being done for the record rather than for safety$qb$, $qb$Staff may need reminding that the checklist exists for their own protection$qb$, $qb$The checklist is probably fine, since the work is being completed anyway$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-024$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A call centre reduces average call time, but repeat calls from the same customers go up. What has likely happened?$qb$, $qb$Calls are being ended before the customer's problem is actually resolved$qb$, $qb$The measure being improved is not the one that matters to the customer$qb$, $qb$Staff are being rewarded for speed rather than for solving the problem$qb$, $qb$Customers may be calling back about new and unrelated problems instead$qb$, $qb$The reduction in call time is a real gain and the repeats are a separate issue$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-025$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A small factory has a machine that jams about once a day. Operators have become skilled at clearing it quickly. What is the risk here?$qb$, $qb$The skill at clearing jams hides how much time and output are being lost$qb$, $qb$Nobody is likely to investigate the jams while they are being handled well$qb$, $qb$The daily jams are being treated as normal rather than as a fault to fix$qb$, $qb$Operators may injure themselves clearing the machine under time pressure$qb$, $qb$The machine may eventually break down completely without any warning$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-026$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A shop assistant suggests moving a display, and the supervisor says the layout was set by head office. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what the layout is meant to achieve, then see if the suggestion serves it better$qb$, $qb$Find out whether head office allows any local changes to the standard layout$qb$, $qb$Try the change for a week and compare sales before and after the move$qb$, $qb$Pass the suggestion up to head office and wait for them to decide on it$qb$, $qb$Keep the layout as set, since head office designed it with more information$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-027$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A hotel gets repeated complaints about slow check-in, but only between four and six in the evening. What does the timing tell you?$qb$, $qb$The problem is probably about capacity at peak times, not about staff speed$qb$, $qb$Something specific happens in those hours that does not happen at other times$qb$, $qb$The check-in process may work fine until a certain number of guests arrive$qb$, $qb$Staff working the late afternoon shift may need more training than others$qb$, $qb$The complaints may simply come from guests who arrive tired and impatient$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-028$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team is asked to make a process faster. Nobody has asked why the process exists. What should happen first?$qb$, $qb$Ask what the process is for, since some steps may not be needed at all$qb$, $qb$Map the whole process before deciding which parts to try to speed up$qb$, $qb$Ask which steps customers actually notice and value in the finished result$qb$, $qb$Find the slowest step and start by looking at how to shorten it$qb$, $qb$Start with the easiest steps to speed up, so early progress is visible$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-029$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A gym notices that most new members stop coming after about six weeks. What is the most useful thing to find out?$qb$, $qb$What happens around week six that makes staying feel harder than starting$qb$, $qb$What the members who did keep coming did differently in their first weeks$qb$, $qb$Whether members who leave say anything about why when they cancel$qb$, $qb$Whether six weeks is normal for gyms generally or unusual for this one$qb$, $qb$Whether the gym could offer a discount to members reaching week six$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-030$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A print shop finds errors are caught at the final check, after the work is already printed. What is the main problem?$qb$, $qb$The errors are being found at the point where fixing them costs the most$qb$, $qb$There is no earlier check, so mistakes travel all the way through the job$qb$, $qb$The final check is doing work that an earlier step should have done$qb$, $qb$The staff doing the final check may need more time to do it properly$qb$, $qb$The final check is working well, since the errors are being caught before delivery$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-031$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two branches of the same bank handle the same task in different ways, and both work. What is the most useful conclusion?$qb$, $qb$There may be something worth learning in the difference between the two$qb$, $qb$One of the two ways may be better, and it would be worth comparing them$qb$, $qb$The task may allow more flexibility than the written procedure suggests$qb$, $qb$The branches should be brought into line so the process is consistent$qb$, $qb$Both branches are getting results, so the difference does not really matter$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-032$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A farm worker points out that a gate is in an awkward place, and has done so for years. Nothing has changed. What does this suggest?$qb$, $qb$There may be no clear route for a suggestion like this to reach a decision$qb$, $qb$The suggestion may never have been recorded anywhere after being raised$qb$, $qb$The cost of moving the gate may have been assumed rather than checked$qb$, $qb$The worker may need to raise it with someone more senior than before$qb$, $qb$The gate is probably fine where it is, or it would have been moved already$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-033$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A supermarket wants to reduce waste in the bakery. Staff say the ovens are the problem. What should you check first?$qb$, $qb$Whether the waste is unsold bread or spoiled ingredients, since these differ$qb$, $qb$How much waste there actually is and at which point in the day it occurs$qb$, $qb$Whether other supermarkets with the same ovens have the same waste levels$qb$, $qb$Whether the ovens are being operated correctly by all of the bakery staff$qb$, $qb$Whether replacing the ovens would cost less than the waste being produced$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-MCQ-034$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team keeps a report that takes two days a month to produce. Nobody can say who uses it. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Ask the people who receive it what decision the report helps them make$qb$, $qb$Stop the report for two months and see whether anyone asks for it$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager whether the report is still needed in its current form$qb$, $qb$Shorten the report so it takes less time while still being available$qb$, $qb$Keep producing it, since a long-standing report probably serves some purpose$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-035$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You work in a busy kitchen. Every evening the team runs short of clean serving dishes during the rush. Staff have started washing dishes by hand to keep going. Nobody has raised it because the team always manages in the end. How do you handle this?$qb$, $qb$Ask what changed, since the team is now absorbing a problem that used to not exist$qb$, $qb$Note how often it happens and how much time it costs, then take that to the manager$qb$, $qb$Raise it with the manager, since the team is covering a problem rather than solving it$qb$, $qb$Suggest buying more dishes, since running short is clearly the immediate problem to fix$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since the team copes well and the service to customers is not suffering$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-036$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are new on a factory line. You notice a step where each worker checks the same thing the previous worker already checked. When you ask why, you are told it has always been done that way. How do you proceed?$qb$, $qb$Find out what problem the second check was added to catch, and whether it still occurs$qb$, $qb$Watch for a week to see whether the second check ever finds anything the first missed$qb$, $qb$Ask the supervisor whether the first check is reliable enough to drop the second one$qb$, $qb$Suggest removing the second check, since it is clearly repeating work already done$qb$, $qb$Leave it alone, since long-standing checks usually exist for some good reason$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-037$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague at the clinic reception has invented a small paper system to track which patients still need follow-up calls. It works well but exists only on her desk. She has never told anyone about it. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Ask what the official system fails to do, since that gap is the real thing to fix$qb$, $qb$Ask whether other staff keep their own versions, which would show how wide the gap is$qb$, $qb$Suggest she shows it to the supervisor, as others may be struggling with the same thing$qb$, $qb$Suggest she keeps using it, since it works and changing it now might cause disruption$qb$, $qb$Suggest she stops, since an unofficial system could cause confusion if she is away$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-038$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You manage a small team in a garden centre. Sales of one product line have fallen steadily for four months. Two staff blame the weather, one blames the price, and one blames the shelf position. How do you find out?$qb$, $qb$Look at what changed four months ago, since a steady fall usually has a starting point$qb$, $qb$Compare the fall with other garden centres, to see whether it is local or more general$qb$, $qb$Test one explanation at a time, starting with the one that is cheapest to check$qb$, $qb$Act on all four explanations at once, so that the sales decline is addressed quickly$qb$, $qb$Ask the staff to vote on which of the four explanations they think is most likely$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-039$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You work in a call centre. A customer describes a problem that you have heard from three other callers this week. There is no way to log that these calls are related. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Raise both things: the repeated fault, and the fact that nothing links related calls$qb$, $qb$Keep your own note of the pattern and take it to your team leader with the details$qb$, $qb$Ask colleagues whether they have had similar calls, to see how widespread this is$qb$, $qb$Log each call in the usual way, since the system will show the pattern eventually$qb$, $qb$Handle each call as it comes, since spotting patterns is not really part of your role$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-040$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A regular customer at your hardware shop mentions that she buys a certain part elsewhere because you never have it. You check and find it has been out of stock for months. How do you take this forward?$qb$, $qb$Find out why it was never reordered, since the same gap may be hiding other lost sales$qb$, $qb$Check whether other customers have asked for this part over the last few months$qb$, $qb$Raise it with whoever manages stock, explaining that a customer has gone elsewhere$qb$, $qb$Order the part now, so that it is available the next time that she comes in$qb$, $qb$Apologise to the customer and suggest she orders it in advance from now on$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-041$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team is asked to cut costs. Everyone starts suggesting things to remove. You think some of the cost comes from rework rather than from the work itself. How do you raise this?$qb$, $qb$Show how much time goes into fixing errors, so rework is treated as a cost to cut$qb$, $qb$Suggest looking at where work has to be redone before deciding what to remove$qb$, $qb$Ask whether anyone has measured how often work comes back needing correction$qb$, $qb$Suggest cutting the rework, since it is clearly waste rather than useful work$qb$, $qb$Go along with the list, since the team has already started work on removing costs$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-042$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are covering a colleague's job for two weeks. You find several steps that seem unnecessary, but you do not know the history behind them. How do you handle what you have noticed?$qb$, $qb$Write down each step and what it seems to achieve, so the question can be asked properly$qb$, $qb$Ask others who know the job whether these steps still serve any current purpose$qb$, $qb$Keep doing the job as instructed and raise your observations when the cover ends$qb$, $qb$Skip the steps that seem unnecessary, since you can see they add nothing useful$qb$, $qb$Say nothing, since you are only covering and the job is not really yours to change$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-043$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A member of your team brings you an idea that would save time but would need another department to change how they work. You think the idea is good. How do you take it forward?$qb$, $qb$Check whether the other department has the same problem, since a shared one is easier to fix$qb$, $qb$Work out what they would gain or lose, before approaching them with the suggestion$qb$, $qb$Talk to them early, so their view shapes the idea before it becomes fixed$qb$, $qb$Send the idea over and ask them to consider making the change at their end$qb$, $qb$Keep the idea inside your team, since involving another department will only slow it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-044$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$The company introduces a new system. Six weeks in, most of your team is still using the old spreadsheet alongside it. Nobody has complained about the new system. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Ask what the spreadsheet still does that the system does not, since that is the real gap$qb$, $qb$Ask the team directly why they are keeping the old spreadsheet going alongside it$qb$, $qb$Report to the project team that the old spreadsheet is still in daily use here$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to stop using the spreadsheet, since the new system has replaced it$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since the team is managing and the work is all still being completed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-045$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that your workplace has three separate forms that all collect the customer's address. Each is owned by a different team. How do you approach this?$qb$, $qb$Find out what each team does with it, since three uses may point to one missing record$qb$, $qb$Ask whether the address could be collected once and then shared between the forms$qb$, $qb$Raise it with the three teams together, so the overlap becomes visible to them all$qb$, $qb$Suggest merging the forms into one, since asking three times wastes everyone's time$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since each team presumably has its own reason for collecting the address$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-046$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier delivers late about one time in four. Your team has quietly started ordering everything a week early to cope. The manager is not aware of this. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Show the manager the late rate and what the early ordering costs, so both are visible$qb$, $qb$Keep a record of the late deliveries for a month, so the problem can be shown clearly$qb$, $qb$Raise the supplier's record with the manager, since the team absorbs their failure$qb$, $qb$Suggest the team keeps ordering early, since it works and avoids a hard conversation$qb$, $qb$Leave it as it is, since the team has found a way to work around the problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-047$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are asked to improve a process, and you find the biggest delay is caused by waiting for one person's approval. That person is senior to you. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Show where the wait sits and ask what would make that approval easier to give quickly$qb$, $qb$Present the waiting time as a fact about the process, not as a fault of that person$qb$, $qb$Ask whether some approvals could be handled by someone else or by an agreed limit$qb$, $qb$Raise it with your own manager and ask them to take it up with the senior person$qb$, $qb$Look at the other steps instead, since the approval step is difficult to change$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-048$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues have each built a tool to solve the same problem, without knowing about each other. Both tools work. How do you handle this?$qb$, $qb$Look at what both tools do, since the same need twice points to a missing feature$qb$, $qb$Ask why neither knew about the other, since the same waste is likely repeating elsewhere$qb$, $qb$Bring them together to compare the tools and agree on one to use going forward$qb$, $qb$Choose the better of the two tools and ask the whole team to adopt that one$qb$, $qb$Let both continue, since each person is comfortable with the tool they built$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-049$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team meets weekly to discuss the same recurring complaint. Each week a temporary fix is agreed. The complaint returns the following week. How do you break the cycle?$qb$, $qb$Point out that the fixes never hold, so the cause sits somewhere nobody has looked$qb$, $qb$Suggest the meeting spends one session on why it returns, rather than on this week$qb$, $qb$Track the complaint over several weeks so the pattern is clear to everyone present$qb$, $qb$Suggest a stronger temporary fix, so the complaint stops coming back each week$qb$, $qb$Continue with the weekly fixes, since they do resolve the complaint each time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PII-SJT-050$qb$, $qb$A2-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You spot a way to improve something outside your own area. You do not know the people involved and you are fairly junior. How do you proceed?$qb$, $qb$Check whether it is already known, then describe the problem rather than your solution$qb$, $qb$Find someone who works in that area and ask whether the issue is a real one there$qb$, $qb$Raise it with your own manager and ask how best to pass it to the right team$qb$, $qb$Send the suggestion straight to the team, since a good idea should stand on its own$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since it is outside your area and the team there will know it better$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
